    <node id="&quot;POLYETHER-POLYURETHANE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyether-Polyurethane Foam is a type of plastic that is being studied for its biodegradation in yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane foam is a type of plastic that has been found to be biodegradable by Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane foam is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms, such as Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane foam is a type of plastic that has been found to be biodegradable by certain insects, such as Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Yellow mealworms, also known as Tenebrio molitor, are a species of insect larvae that possess a unique ability to biodegrade various types of plastics. They are capable of ingesting and degrading different layers of surgical masks, with varying preferences for each layer, and their gut microbiome is affected by the ingestion of different layers of masks. The gut of yellow mealworms contains microorganisms that play a crucial role in the degradation of these plastics, contributing to the breakdown of nanoplastic particles and degrading plastics such as polystyrene foam and polyurethane foam. Yellow mealworms have been found to be capable of growing on a diet of polyether-PU foam alone and have a high nutritional value due to their high protein content, making them a potential feed ingredient. They have a life cycle that includes four stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. Yellow mealworms are being used to study the biodegradation of polypropylene masks, with different survival rates and frass production observed across various experimental groups. They are also used as a model organism to study the effects of different diets on the gut microbiome, and their gut fungal diversity increases significantly after feeding on different layers of masks. Overall, yellow mealworms are a valuable species in the context of environmental sustainability, with a range of potential applications in the field of plastic biodegradation, and are capable of degrading various types of plastics, including polyethylene, polypropylene, polyurethane, polyethylene, and PVC. They are the main subject of several studies, where their ability to consume and degrade plastics is being investigated, and changes in their gut bacterial community in response to different diets are being observed. Yellow mealworms are insects that can biodegrade plastics, with a gut microbiome that plays a crucial role in the biodegradation process, and they are capable of biodegrading polystyrene and polypropylene via gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization, making them a promising solution for reducing plastic waste.</data>
