    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor, also known as the mealworm, mealworm beetle, yellow mealworm, or darkling beetle, is a species of insect that possesses unique abilities to biodegrade various types of waste and plastics. It is a species of beetle that is being studied for its developmental plasticity and its ability to degrade polystyrene. Tenebrio molitor has unique energy requirements and reserve dynamics, making it an interesting subject for research. The larvae of Tenebrio molitor, known as mealworms or yellow mealworms, can eat and degrade a wide range of plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, polypropylene, polyvinyl chloride, polylactic acid, polyether-polyurethane foam, and cellulose, as well as expanded polystyrene and low-density polyethylene. The gut environment of Tenebrio molitor is capable of rapid plastic biodegradation, making it an important area of study. The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a crucial role in degrading plastics, with its gut-associated bacteria, such as Klebsiella sp. and Cellulosimicrobium sp., being studied for their potential in biodegradation. Tenebrio molitor has been found to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene plastics, and can degrade HDPE with a mass loss of approximately 32% after one month of exposure. Additionally, the larvae of Tenebrio molitor have been found to be capable of surviving on PVC with a reduced body weight loss compared to starvation. Tenebrio molitor is also being researched for its potential as a protein source and its ability to degrade plastic, making it a subject of interest in scientific research. Furthermore, Tenebrio molitor is being studied for its potential use as a food source, particularly as a suitable alternative protein source for broiler and pig feed, due to its high protein content. The insect's larvae are used to biodegrade polyurethane foam, resulting in a significant degradation. Overall, Tenebrio molitor has shown significant potential in biodegrading microplastics and other types of plastics, and its unique characteristics make it an important species for further study and research. As a species of mealworm, Tenebrio molitor can be used as a source of nutrients, and its capabilities are being studied extensively to enhance its potential for selective breeding</data>
