    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays an important role in their ability to adapt to different foods and degrade various types of waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of mealworms is composed of microorganisms that play a crucial role in the decomposition of plastics, including biodegradable and non-biodegradable materials."&lt;SEP&gt;The GUT MICROBIOME refers to the community of microorganisms living in the gut of various organisms, including animals, insects, and insect larvae, such as mealworms, also known as Tenebrio molitor or yellow mealworms, as well as other insects like Galleria Mellonella and the greater wax moth. The GUT MICROBIOME plays a crucial role in the health and development of these organisms, as well as in their ability to degrade various substances, including plastics, such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and polypropylene. Specifically, the GUT MICROBIOME of mealworms is being analyzed to understand its role in degrading PS polymers, with different microbial communities responding to various polymer molecular weights. The GUT MICROBIOME of mealworms is involved in the depolymerization of PS, the biodegradation of polyethylene and plastic mixtures, and the degradation of surgical masks. The community of microorganisms in the GUT MICROBIOME can vary significantly in response to different diets, with changes in bacterial and fungal diversities observed when ingesting different materials. Certain species of bacteria, such as Kluyvera sp. and Pedicoccus sp., contribute to the breakdown of plastic polymers like polypropylene. The GUT MICROBIOME of Tenebrio molitor larvae contributes significantly to PET biodegradation, which is considered a symbiotic activity between the gut microbes and the host. Overall, the GUT MICROBIOME is a vital component of the digestive system of mealworms and other organisms, and its unique composition and function make it an important area of study in the field of biodegradation, with its role in the degradation of plastics and other substances being vital for understanding the complex interactions between the GUT MICROBIOME, the host organism, and the environment. Additionally, the GUT MICROBIOME plays a crucial role in the health and development of animals, highlighting its importance in the broader context of organismal biology.</data>
