    <node id="&quot;FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNIQUE"</data>
      <data key="d1">FTIR, or Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, is a versatile analytical technique used by researchers to analyze the chemical composition and structure of various materials, including plastics. FTIR is utilized to investigate the changes of functional groups in polymers, such as polyurethane and polystyrene, and to characterize the functional groups of extracted residual polymers. This technique is also used to confirm the biodegradation and oxidation of plastics, such as polystyrene and low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and to test the biodegradation of plastics. FTIR is employed to analyze the molecular structure of substances, including microplastics and their degradation products. Additionally, FTIR is used to analyze the composition of materials, including the chemical composition of frass, polystyrene microplastics, and polyether-polyurethane foam, as well as polyethylene plastics and polyethylene terephthalate polymers. FTIR is used to examine the composition of mealworm fecula and compare it to the polystyrene feedstock, and to study the degradation of polyethylene by yellow mealworms. Furthermore, FTIR is used to analyze the surface morphology and functional groups of polystyrene/cellulose samples. FTIR provides valuable insights into the properties and behavior of various materials, including the formation of new oxidative functional groups in polymers. As a non-invasive technique, FTIR is a tool used to analyze the chemical structure of materials, providing comprehensive information about the chemical composition and structure of various substances. FTIR is widely used to analyze the molecular structure of plastics and other materials, and to characterize the functional groups of polystyrene residues, revealing changes in the molecular structure of the plastic. By using FTIR, researchers can gain a deeper understanding of the properties and behavior of materials, including the degradation of Mi-PS and other substances. Overall, FTIR is a biochemical measure that is used to analyze the structure of polystyrene and its degradation by the microbiota, and it is a technique used to analyze the chemical structure of polymers, including the formation of new oxidative functional groups, and the physiochemical properties of substances, including the biodegradation of polyethylene waste, and the biodegradation of polyethylene in larvae of certain species. FTIR is also used to characterize the formation of oxidized functional groups in plastics, and to analyze the chemical composition of LDPE polymers and its biodegradation</data>
