    <node id="&quot;GALLERIA MELLONELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Galleria mellonella, also known as the greater wax moth, is a type of microorganism that can degrade certain types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, also known as the greater wax moth or waxworms, is a species of insect that possesses a unique ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene, polystyrene, and polypropylene. The larvae of Galleria mellonella have a unique ability to break down and modify the backbone of these plastics, and their biodegradation potential is influenced by their intestinal microorganisms, including Enterobacter sp. and Masilia sp. FS1903. Galleria mellonella has a gut microbiome capable of biodegrading polyethylene, and the species has been used to study plastic biodegradation and is used as a model organism in scientific studies. Additionally, Galleria mellonella is capable of metabolizing long-chain hydrocarbon beeswax, and its hologenome supports microbiota-independent metabolism. The species' larvae can ingest and consume plastic diets containing recalcitrant plastics, and they can chew and eat various plastic packaging materials, including polyethylene films. Galleria mellonella's ability to degrade plastics makes it a subject of interest in the field of biodegradation. The species is often compared to Tenebrio molitor in terms of biodegradation. Overall, Galleria mellonella is a valuable model organism for studying plastic biodegradation, and its larvae have been reported to consume and potentially biodegrade polyethylene, polystyrene, and other plastics, making it a significant species in the study of plastic degradation. Galleria mellonella can also host Enterobacter sp. D1, and its caterpillars are capable of biodegrading polyethylene. The species has been found to biodegrade polyolefins, with changes in microbial composition, and has been used in studies to investigate the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly in the context of microbiome research. Furthermore, Galleria mellonella has been found to have polystyrene-degrading bacteria in its gut, highlighting its potential in biodegrading various types of plastics, including microplastics. It is worth noting that Galleria mellonella may experience oxidative stress due to its ability to degrade microplastics. Different diets have been shown to affect the activity and biodegradation rates of Galleria mellonella, making it a fascinating subject for further research. As a species of moth, Galleria mellonella</data>
