    <node id="&quot;ACHATINA FULICA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Achatina fulica is a genus of snails that can take up polyethylene terephthalate microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a type of snail that has been found to have microorganisms in its gut that can break down plastic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, a land snail, has been discovered to disintegrate polystyrene (PS), showcasing the diverse potential for biodegradation in nature."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as the land snail, is a species of snail that can biodegrade expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as the land snail, is an insect that can biodegrade expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as the snail, is an insect that can ingest and consume plastic diets containing recalcitrant plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as land snails, are animals that can ingest and degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a species of land snail that has been found to biodegrade expanded polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as land snails, have been reported to eat plastics, including polyethylene and polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a species of land snail that is mentioned as a comparison to Tenebrio molitor in terms of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as the land snail, is an insect that can biodegrade expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as the land snail, is a microorganism capable of biodegrading expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a species of land snail that can biodegrade microplastics and is used in studies to understand the effects of microplastics on the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a species of terrestrial snail that can fragment commercial PS foam into large microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, also known as the terrestrial snail, is an organism that is being studied for its ability to biodegrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a species of land snails capable of biodegrading expanded polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e</data>
