    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene, also known as PE, is a type of synthetic polymer derived from petroleum, widely used in the production of plastics. It has various types, including HDPE, LLDPE, and LDPE, each with distinct properties and characteristics. Polyethylene is characterized as a type of thermoplastic with a surface consisting of flat valleys and flaky layers of material superimposed on one another. It is known for its absorption bands in its FT-IR spectrum, including C-H stretching and bending vibrations, and has a density of approximately 0.92 g/cm^3. Polyethylene is commonly used in packaging and other applications. Initially, Polyethylene was considered difficult to biodegrade due to its high molecular weight, hydrophobicity, and inert covalent bonds, as well as its lack of hydrolytic functional groups and stable 'C-C' bonds. However, Polyethylene has been found to be biodegradable by various microorganisms, including certain bacterial strains, such as Enterobacter sp. D1, Priestia megaterium S1, Enterobacter absburiae strain YT1, and Bacillus sp. YP1, as well as insect larvae, including the yellow mealworm, Tenebrio molitor, Tenebrio obscurus, the greater wax moth larvae, superworms, and the lesser waxworm. The biodegradation of Polyethylene by these organisms involves the activity of esterases and other enzymes, resulting in the oxidation and depolymerization of the plastic, with a decrease in substrate mass and changes in its chemical structure and molecular weight over time. Additionally, Polyethylene can be degraded by other microorganisms, including Galleria mellonella, Achroia Grisella, and the strain PELW2042, as well as Rhodococcus ruber and Klebsiella Pneumoniae Mk-1, and by phanerochaete and streptomyces species. Polyethylene can also be degraded in a compost environment, indicating its potential for biodegradation. Furthermore, Polyethylene can be biodepolymerized by Tenebrio molitor and biodegraded by mealworms, which are also known as Tenebrio molitor larvae. It is also worth noting that Polyethylene is degraded by Pseudomonas through the alkB gene. Polyethylene is subject to bacterial colonization processes in landfill sites, where microorganisms play</data>
