    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene is a type of plastic that is widely used for packaging and insulation, but it is hardly degradable and resistant to photo-oxidation. It is a polymeric material, also known as PS or styrofoam, that is commonly used in packaging materials, building, and food processing. Polystyrene is a synthetic plastic with a long hydrocarbon chain and a phenyl group attached to every other carbon atom, synthesized from styrene monomers by a free radical polycondensation reaction, with a chemical expression of [-CH(C6H5)CH2 - ]n. Despite being one of the most widely produced plastics in the world, Polystyrene is known for its environmental persistence and toxicity, making it one of the most hazardous plastics to the environment and human health. Additionally, Polystyrene is a toxic substance that can affect the growth and development of T. molitor larvae, and it is not a sufficient source of mass and energy for these larvae. However, Polystyrene has been found to be biodegradable by certain microorganisms and insects, including mealworms, yellow mealworms, Zophobas atratus, Tenebrio molitor, Tenebrio obscurus, Galleria mellonella, the greater wax moth larvae, and black soldier fly. The biodegradation process of Polystyrene involves oxidation and depolymerization in the larval guts of certain insects, with the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor playing a significant role in the biodegradation of Polystyrene. Certain microorganisms, such as Pseudomonas sp., Klebsiella sp. WJ2020, Cellulosimicrobium sp. WJ2025, Acinetobacter, Rhodococcus ruber, Priestia megaterium S1, Kocuria, and other bacterial strains, as well as soil bacteria and microorganisms like LG3 and SCGB1, are also capable of degrading Polystyrene. The biodegradation of Polystyrene by these microorganisms and insects can occur through various processes, including enzymatic activity, anaerobic processes, and the action of diazotrophs. While Polystyrene consumption can cause high mortality in the insects and affect their nutritional value and oxidative stress parameters, ongoing research on the biodegradation and recycling possibilities of Polystyrene aims to find</data>
