    <node id="&quot;POLYVINYL CHLORIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyvinyl Chloride, also known as PVC, is a type of plastic that is widely used in various industries and daily life, making up 10.4% of plastic production and 10.4% of thermoplastics. It is chemically expressed as [-CHClCH2 - ]n and is one of the most commonly used petroleum-based polymers. Polyvinyl Chloride is commonly used in packaging, construction, medical devices, pipes, and other applications. Despite being a non-hydrolysable polymer, Polyvinyl Chloride is being studied for its potential to be biodegraded by certain microorganisms and insects, such as the yellow mealworm and Tenebrio molitor. In fact, Tenebrio molitor, also known as the mealworm, has been found to be capable of biodegrading Polyvinyl Chloride, with its larvae able to ingest and consume the plastic. The biodegradation process of Polyvinyl Chloride by mealworms has a significant impact on the nutritional value and mortality rate of the mealworms. Polyvinyl Chloride is less favorable for mealworm consumption, with lower average consumption rates compared to polystyrene and polypropylene, at an average consumption rate of 0.00249 mg per day per mealworm. However, certain insects, such as Ephesia cautella and Plodia interpunctella, can penetrate Polyvinyl Chloride. Additionally, Polyvinyl Chloride can be degraded by certain microorganisms, including those found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, making it a material of interest for further study and potential applications in plastic waste management. While it was initially thought to be resistant to microbial degradation and enzymatic attack, studies have shown that certain species of insects and microorganisms can indeed break down Polyvinyl Chloride, similar to the mechanisms of polyethylene degradation. Overall, Polyvinyl Chloride is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms and insects, and its biodegradation is an area of ongoing research. It is also one of the most hazardous plastics to the environment and human health, highlighting the need for further research into its degradation and potential applications in reducing plastic waste. 

Note: The initial description stating that Polyvinyl Chloride is a biodegradable plastic and the description stating that it is non-biodegradable have been reconciled, as the majority of the descriptions and research suggest</data>
