    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms, specifically the larvae of Zophobas atratus, are capable of biodegrading and mineralizing polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, also known as Zophobas atratus or Zophobas morio, are insects that possess the unique ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene, polystyrene, and polypropylene, through a gut-microbe-dependent process. They are capable of consuming these plastics at a significant rate, with some studies showing that they can eat Styrofoam as their sole diet at a rate of 0.58 mg/d per superworm. Superworms have a gut microbiota that contributes to polystyrene degradation, and their nutritional composition can be affected by consuming polystyrene. They are being studied for their potential applications in waste management and sustainable bioremediation, as they can degrade and mineralize plastics, including polystyrene. Superworms are also being compared to mealworms in terms of their ability to consume polystyrene and their potential effects on cell cytotoxicity. Furthermore, Superworms are capable of producing frass, which can be used as a medium for plant growth, such as supporting cacti growth. Overall, Superworms are a type of insect that can be used as a food source and have potential applications in a zero-waste circular economy, due to their ability to consume and degrade plastic waste. They are a subject of study for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene, and their activities are supported by the consumption of a Styrofoam diet. Superworms are a promising solution for the degradation of plastic waste, and their unique abilities make them an important area of research in the field of bioremediation. They can survive on a diet of plastics, including polystyrene and polyurethane, and have a life cycle that includes four stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. With a survival rate of 100% in both plastic-fed and control bran-fed groups, Superworms are a fascinating insect species that can biodegrade polypropylene through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization, making them a valuable asset in the fight against plastic waste. Additionally, Superworms are known to digest polystyrene and polyethylene materials, and are also referred to as Zophobas aratus. They are a type of insect that is used in studies to analyze their ability to consume Styrofoam, and their ability to biodegrade polypropylene via gut</data>
