    <node id="&quot;LDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE, or low-density polyethylene, is a type of plastic that can be degraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE, or low-density polyethylene, is a type of plastic used in the study to investigate biodegradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;LDPE, or Low-Density Polyethylene, is a type of plastic polymer that has been found to be biodegradable by certain microorganisms, including Enterobacteriaceae, Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9, and Priestia megaterium S1, as well as insects such as Tenebrio molitor larvae and Z. atratus. It is a type of polyethylene with high long chain branching and low crystallinity, making it suitable for specific applications and affecting its degradability. LDPE is commonly used in packaging and other applications, and its biodegradation is being studied by various methods, including its thermal properties, degradation, and potential applications, as well as its pyrolysis and use as a petro-chemical feedstock. LDPE can be biodegraded by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae, with evidence of biodegradation and oxidation provided by FTIR analysis. The biodegradation of LDPE is mediated by enzymes such as CES and GSTs, and it has a linear chain and branching structure, which makes it preferable to mealworms over other types of plastic, such as LLDPE. LDPE undergoes broad depolymerization, showing significant reduction of molecular weight and suggesting more rapid depolymerization than LLDPE and HDPE. Additionally, LDPE has been found to have an average consumption rate of 0.00102 mg per day per mealworm, and its presence may affect the fatty acid degradation pathway and potentially be toxic to the mealworms. LDPE can also undergo oxidative degradation. While some studies suggest that LDPE is difficult to biodegrade due to its high molecular weight and hydrophobicity, it is clear that LDPE can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms and insects. Overall, LDPE is an active area of research, with its molecular weights determined by High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography, and its comparison to other types of plastic, such as PET and HDPE, in terms of biodegradation by T. molitor larvae, is also being explored.</data>
