    <node id="&quot;PP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP (polypropylene) is a type of plastic that is found in some animal-based traditional medicinal materials, indicating the presence of microplastics in these materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP is a type of material that has not been tested for biodegradation by T. obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;PP, or polypropylene, is a type of plastic material that has been studied for its biodegradability by various organisms, including mealworms, superworms, and certain species of Bacillus. It is a semicrystalline plastic that can be ingested and consumed by certain insects, such as the yellow mealworm and T. molitor larvae. The biodegradation of PP is mediated by enzymes such as CES and GSTs, and it is also associated with various bacterial communities in these insects. PP has been compared to other plastics, such as PET, in terms of biodegradation by T. molitor larvae. Additionally, it has been found that certain microorganisms, including the gut microbial community of T. molitor, can degrade PP. While PP has a low cumulative consumption by mealworms, it is still considered a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by these organisms. PP is also being studied for its biodegradation rate and extent, and it has been used as a control in biodegradability experiments. Furthermore, PP is a type of non-hydrolyzable plastic that is more resistant to biodegradation than hydrolyzable polymers like PET. However, it can be degraded by bacteria and has a significant effect on mealworm mass, with mealworms consuming PP having a higher mass gain than those consuming PS. PP is also being studied for its degradation by yellow mealworms, with an average consumption rate of 0.0125 mg per day per mealworm. It is a type of plastic polymer associated with biodegradation and is being researched for its potential to be biodegraded by various organisms. Overall, PP is a type of plastic that is being researched for its potential to be biodegraded by various organisms, and its biodegradation is an active area of study.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006