    <node id="&quot;PVC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">PVC, or polyvinyl chloride, is a type of petroleum-based plastic that is composed of polyvinyl chloride and various additives, such as plasticizers, flame retardants, anti-aging agents, adhesives, and chlorides. PVC is a non-biodegradable material, but research has shown that it can be ingested and consumed by certain microorganisms and insects, including mealworms like the yellow mealworm and Tenebrio molitor larvae. Mealworms have been found to show a low mass loss when fed PVC, indicating that PVC is not an efficient source of energy for them. However, PVC can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae with a mass reduction rate of 6.7 ± 5.0 mg/100 larvae-d, and its degradation is mediated by enzymes such as CES and GSTs. The degradation of PVC by mealworms results in changes to its crystalline structure and the appearance of new functional groups. PVC contains functional groups such as carboxyl and hydroxyl, and a distinguishing chloride group that may cause fluctuations in mealworm mass. PVC is being studied for its pyrolysis and potential to form toxic compounds, as well as its biodegradation rate and extent. Comparisons have been made between PVC and other plastics, such as PET, in terms of biodegradation by T. molitor larvae, and research has shown that PVC can be depolymerized and biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor, with a reduction in molecular weight. PVC has a number average molecular weight of 68.4 kDa, which is less preferred by Tenebrio molitor larvae for degradation. The removal efficiency of PVC by larvae fed with plastics only is 58.2%, and 49.6% by larvae fed with plastics plus bran. While PVC is considered a type of polymer with low degradability, it is being used as a food source for plastic-degrading larvae, and its degradation is an area of ongoing research. Mealworms can consume PVC at an average rate of 0.00249 mg per day per mealworm. Although PVC is more resistant to biodegradation than hydrolyzable polymers like PET, it can still be biodegraded by mealworms, indicating that PVC is not entirely non-degradable as initially thought. Overall, PVC is a complex material that is being studied for its biodegradation and potential environmental</data>
