    <node id="&quot;T. MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">T. MOLITOR, also known as the yellow mealworm or Tenebrio molitor, is a species of insect from the Tenebrionidae family, found in temperate climate regions. T. MOLITOR is a type of beetle that is being studied for its life cycle, growth patterns, and response to different diets. The insect is specifically a species of mealworm larvae that is being studied for its growth and survival patterns under different dietary conditions. T. MOLITOR is capable of consuming and breaking down various materials, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyether-PU foam, lignin, cellulose, and hemicellulose, as well as polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polypropylene (PP). The insect's gut microbiome is capable of degrading these substances, with numerous bacterial species in its digestive tract producing esterases that aid in decomposition. Specifically, its digestive tract contains a variety of bacteria, including Bacillus, Pantoea, and Erwinia, which can generate biofilms on polystyrene surfaces. T. MOLITOR has a symbiotic relationship with bacteria in its digestive tract, which helps in the decomposition of polystyrene. The insect is commonly used as a model organism for studying symbiosis and nitrogen fixation. T. MOLITOR is capable of consuming and breaking down plastic materials, such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foams, expanded polystyrene foam, and polyether-PU foam. Its larvae have been used to analyze their gut microbial community and ability to consume Styrofoam, as well as their ability to degrade polystyrene. The larvae are fed with wheat bran and later with Styrofoam blocks, and their gut microbiome is associated with PS degradation. T. MOLITOR's larvae can consume and degrade LDPE, with a higher specific LDPE consumption rate than some other species. The process of LDPE depolymerization by T. MOLITOR's larvae can be gut-microbe independent. The insect's consumption of plastics has been observed to result in the conversion of carbon to CO2, with around 40% of the carbon being converted to CO2, while approximately 50% of the carbon remains in the frass. Overall, T. MOLITOR plays a significant role in the biodegradation of various materials, and its unique gut micro</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9