    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION MECHANISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradation mechanism is the process by which microorganisms break down complex materials, such as polyether-PU foam, into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biodegradation mechanism of polyether-PU foam involves the action of microorganisms, such as Enterobacteriaceae and Staphylococcus epidermidis."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biodegradation mechanism refers to the process by which microorganisms break down organic substances, such as polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biodegradation mechanism of polyethylene by larvae is not fully understood and requires further study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation mechanism refers to the process by which microorganisms break down plastics into smaller components."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical reaction that involves the breakdown of a molecule using water, potentially contributing to the biodegradation of polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction that occurs when urethane bonds in polyether-PU foam are broken down during mealworm ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a type of chemical reaction that is involved in the biodegradation of polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction that is involved in the metabolism of isoniazid, and is mediated by the enzyme CES."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction that can be used to break down PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction involved in the cleavage of chemical bonds using water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction that involves the breakdown of a molecule using water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a type of chemical reaction that can occur during the biodegradation of polypropylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SCISSION OF ETHER BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Scission of ether bonds is a type of chemical reaction that is involved in the biodegradation of polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Depolymerization is a chemical reaction that breaks down polymers into smaller molecules, and it is an important step in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization is a chemical reaction that occurs during the biodegradation of plastics, resulting in the breakdown of polymer chains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization is a chemical reaction that occurs during the biodegradation of plastics, resulting in the breakdown of polymers into smaller molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization is the process by which polymers are broken down into smaller components, such as monomers."&lt;SEP&gt;DEPOLYMERIZATION is a process that involves the breakdown of polymer chains, which is essential for the biodegradation of plastics and other materials. It can occur through both biological and chemical reactions, and is facilitated by the action of microorganisms, such as Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms, and their gut microbiome, as well as by enzymes like CES and GSTs. DEPOLYMERIZATION is a crucial step in the biodegradation of complex materials, including plastics, surgical masks, and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymers, and plays a significant role in the degradation of polypropylene, polyethylene, and polystyrene plastics. This process involves the breakdown of polymer chains into smaller molecules, resulting in a reduction in molecular weight and an increase in hydrophilicity. DEPOLYMERIZATION is observed in the degradation of polystyrene by mealworms and other microorganisms, such as T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae, and is also the process by which microplastics, including PS and PE microplastics, undergo degradation, resulting in a change in molecular weight distribution. It is a vital process that contributes to the degradation of plastics, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and is being studied in research, particularly in the context of yellow mealworms and other organisms that break down plastics into smaller molecules. Overall, DEPOLYMERIZATION is a key step in the biodegradation of plastics, and its understanding is essential for the development of effective strategies for plastic waste management.&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization is a chemical reaction that occurs during the biodegradation of polystyrene, where the polymer chains are broken down into smaller units."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization is the process of breaking down polymer chains, with broad depolymerization (BD) and limited-extent depolymerization (LD) patterns being observed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c0603