    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMATACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is also present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and is associated with the biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is associated with the gut microbiome of Tenebrio species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is dominant in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed with bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is dominant in the gut microbiomes of PET-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family that includes species such as Spiroplasma velicicrescens."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family that increased in relative abundance in the PP-fed group, indicating its close relationship with the PP diet and significant role in PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family associated with LDPE biodegradation in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria present in the gut of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is associated with the gut microbiome of Tenebrio species and is strongly associated with the PS diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is dominant in the gut microbiomes of PET-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed with bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family that includes species such as Enterococcus hirae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is dominant in the gut-bacterial community of the bran-fed group, with a relative abundance of 20.79%."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and is associated with the biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is affected by the presence of MPs in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STREPTOCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family associated with LDPE biodegradation in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria present in the gut of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that includes species potentially involved in the biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that increases in relative abundance in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed with polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family that includes species such as Lactococcus garvieae and Lactococcus taiwanensis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family that includes species such as Lactococcus garvieae, which is involved in EPS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is found in higher relative abundances in the middle-fed and inner-fed groups of yellow mealworms compared to the bran-fed control group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is significantly higher in the middle-fed and inner-fed groups, compared to the bran-fed group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is significantly higher in the middle-fed and inner-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is also predominant in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae and contributes to the biodegradation of aged MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that is promoted by the presence of aged MPs in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that can degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria associated with the biodegradation of low molecular weight polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTERIACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterobacteriaceae is a family of bacteria that is associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly in mealworms fed with medium molecular weight polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae is a bacterial family that belongs to the class Gammaproteobacteria and plays a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics. The family includes species capable of degrading certain plastics, such as high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), polyhydroxyalkanoates (PHA), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyether-polyurethane (PU), polystyrene (PS), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Enterobacteriaceae is a common family found in the gut microbiota of various insect larvae, including Tenebrio molitor, Tenebrio obscurus, and superworms, and is associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene. The family is dominant in the gut microbiome of T. obscurus and is strongly associated with the PS diet. Enterobacteriaceae is also predominant in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio molitor larvae and plays a significant role in the biodegradation of aged microplastics (MPs). The presence of aged MPs in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae promotes the growth of Enterobacteriaceae. The family includes several species capable of growing on plastics, such as Citrobacter sp., Kosakonia sp., and Cronobacter sakazakii, as well as Enterobacter hormaechei. Enterobacteriaceae is significantly more abundant in PS samples than in control samples and is also associated with the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae. Overall, Enterobacteriaceae is a family of bacteria that is capable of breaking down synthetic products and is an important component of the gut microbial community of insects that feed on these materials, making it a possible candidate for plastic consumption.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
