    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene (PS) is a type of plastic that is being tested for its biodegradability by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is a type of synthetic polymer made from the polymerization of styrene monomer, with specific properties such as density and molecular weight. It is widely used in various sectors, particularly in food packaging and building constructions, due to its durability and resistance to biodegradation. Polystyrene (PS) is commonly known as Styrofoam and is considered to be durable and resistant to biodegradation, with good thermal insulation, radiation resistance, and high oil yields from pyrolysis, often used to make thermal insulation materials. However, Polystyrene (PS) is also a major contributor to plastic pollution due to its slow biodegradation rate and low recycling rates, leading to environmental pollution.

Despite its resistance to biodegradation, Polystyrene (PS) can be biodegraded by certain species of insects and microorganisms. Tenebrio molitor larvae, also known as mealworms, can consume and break down the plastic, with aged PS being more efficiently depolymerized and biodegraded than pristine PS. Other insects, such as superworms and the land snail Achatina fulica, can also consume and break down Polystyrene (PS) with varying rates of efficiency. Additionally, certain bacteria, including those within the Enterobacter genus, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, as well as other bacterial strains like Bacillus amyloliquefaciens, Aeromonas sp., and Stenotrophomonas, are capable of degrading Polystyrene (PS). Microorganisms such as Strain LG3 and Strain SCGB1, and various other microorganisms, including those found in the gut of larvae, are also capable of degrading Polystyrene (PS). Priestia megaterium S1 is also known to degrade Polystyrene (PS), and Black Soldier Fly larvae show a strong expression of the purine metabolism pathway when exposed to Polystyrene (PS), indicating its impact on the central carbon metabolism of insect models.

The biodegradation process of Polystyrene (PS) by these organisms results in decreases in molecular weight and changes in functional groups, leading to a significant reduction in molecular weight and an increase in polydispersity index (PDI). Although it lacks nutritional value, Polystyrene (PS) is being consumed by T. molitor larvae and other insects. Pol</data>
