    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYPROPYLENE (PP)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) is a type of plastic that is widely used and has a similar structure to polyethylene (PE). It is a linear hydrocarbon resin with a chemical formula of (C2H4)n, which can form different chain structures, including atactic, isotactic, and syndiotactic. Polypropylene (PP) is a major source of plastic solid wastes and pollutants. Despite its resistance to biodegradation, Polypropylene (PP) can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms, insects, and larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, Z. atratus, yellow mealworms, and superworms. These organisms can consume and break down Polypropylene (PP), with mealworms showing a relatively high compatibility with it as a feeding source. Additionally, certain bacteria have also been found to degrade Polypropylene (PP). Polypropylene (PP) is non-hydrolyzable and resistant to enzymatic depolymerization or degradation, but it can be biodepolymerized by certain organisms. It is commonly used in packaging materials and has been found to have various properties and applications. Polypropylene (PP) can also affect the central carbon metabolism of insect models, with some insects showing a strong expression of the purine metabolism pathway when exposed to it. Overall, Polypropylene (PP) is a complex plastic material that is being studied for its biodegradation patterns and processes, and its interactions with various organisms, making it a potential area of future research on plastic biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a type of petro-based polymer ingested by mealworms, causing oxidative stress and damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms, such as the yellow mealworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a type of plastic that has been found to be biodegradable by certain species of insects, including Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a type of plastic that has been widely used in various applications, including building materials, industrial products, daily necessities, artificial leather, pipes, wires and cables, and packaging films. It is known for its versatility and has a different structure than other types of plastic, such as polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP). While Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is commonly known to be resistant to biodegradation and can cause physiological stress responses in macroinvertebrates, recent studies have shown that it can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms and insects, such as the yellow mealworm, superworms, and the larvae of Tenebrio molitor (TM). Additionally, other species of insects, such as Z. atratus, and fungal isolates have also been found to biodegrade Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC). The biodegradability of Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) by certain insects and microorganisms makes it an interesting subject for further study, and its versatility as a plastic material allows it to be used in a range of applications. Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a complex material with both resistant and biodegradable properties, depending on the context and the organisms involved. Overall, Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a synthetic polymer and a type of plastic that has been widely used and studied, with its consumption by mealworms and biodegradation by various organisms being significant in plastic waste management and biotechnology.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c