      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium known to degrade crude oil and produce a rhamnolipid that acts as a biosurfactant, and is capable of growth on PS alone."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium that can biodegrade polyester polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a type of microorganism that is capable of degrading alkanes, a type of hydrocarbon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium that has been isolated from the gut extract of superworms and has been found to biodegrade various types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium that has been isolated and has shown biodegradable ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium that has been studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene, particularly in the context of microbiome research and environmental cleanup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a species of bacteria that has been shown to degrade polystyrene foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a type of bacterium that has been found to be capable of degrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium used as an outgroup in the phylogenetic analysis of nitrogen-fixing bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium used as an outgroup in the study, with its 16S rRNA sequence used for comparison."</data>
