    <node id="&quot;TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tribolium castaneum is a species of beetle whose larvae are associated with a polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacter bacterium."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is a species of darkling beetle that is capable of biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;Tribolium castaneum, also known as the red flour beetle, is a species of insect that has been extensively studied for its ability to degrade various types of plastics. The species has been found to possess a gut microbial flora that biodegrades polystyrene, and its larvae have been discovered to host Acinetobacter bacteria that can degrade polystyrene. Tribolium castaneum is capable of chewing and eating different plastic packaging materials, including expanded polystyrene (EPS), and has been found to biodegrade plastics such as polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). The insect's ability to degrade plastics makes it a subject of interest in the context of biodegradation and microbiome research. Additionally, Tribolium castaneum has been found to be affected by the management of loose plastic packaging, and its life cycle and weight variations have also been studied. The species is also being researched for its potential to biodegrade other types of plastics, including PET polymers. Overall, Tribolium castaneum is a significant species in the context of plastic biodegradation, and its unique abilities make it an important area of study in the field of environmental science.&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is an insect that has microorganisms in its digestive tract that can degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene degradation is the process by which polystyrene is broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene Degradation is a biological process by which polystyrene is broken down into simpler compounds through the action of microorganisms, including certain insects and bacteria. This process involves the breakdown of polystyrene into smaller components, often using it as a source of carbon, and can be carried out by various microorganisms such as superworms, mealworms, and bacteria like Rhodococcus ruber. The gut microbiota of Tenebrio molitor, a type of mealworm, also plays a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene. Polystyrene Degradation is a potential solution to the problem of plastic waste, as it allows for the breakdown of polystyrene, a type of synthetic plastic polymer, by living organisms. This process has been observed in various studies, including those involving PS samples, and is considered a biological process by which microorganisms break down polystyrene into simpler substances. Overall, Polystyrene Degradation is an important process that can help to reduce the amount of plastic waste in the environment, and further research is needed to fully understand the mechanisms and potential applications of this process.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-126ff1ea4b1c89f82a750386be59f926&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-908b8bfa7e446694958cf9bd0cfbc583&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af</data>
