    <node id="&quot;MICROORGANISMS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57f3782f3c1c49764c4b911c60b37fed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microorganisms are the microorganisms that are capable of degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microorganisms are tiny living organisms that are capable of biodegrading certain types of plastic, including polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microorganisms are tiny living organisms that can break down plastics and other materials through biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Microorganisms are a broad category of living organisms that include bacteria, archaea, fungi, and other microscopic life forms. They are small living organisms that can break down materials, such as plastics, into simpler components through biodegradation. Microorganisms are capable of degrading a wide range of organic matter, including polystyrene, polyethylene, polypropylene, and polyurethane. They play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics and are being studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics, with a focus on the gut bacteria of larvae, such as mealworms and Tenebrio molitor larvae. The microorganisms present in the gut of these larvae contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene and polypropylene and are involved in the biodepolymerization of plastic waste. Specific microorganisms, such as Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides and Paracoccus sp., are being investigated for their ability to biodegrade polyurethane. Microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, are capable of biodegrading polyethylene, with their efficiency affected by the polymer's structure and properties. Although their ability to biodegrade polyethylene is limited, even with pretreatment processes such as photo- or thermo-oxidation of polymers, microorganisms are still a vital component in the breakdown of plastic pollution. They are cultivated on various media and are being researched for their ability to break down biodegradable plastic and conventional plastic. Additionally, microorganisms are involved in nitrogen conversion and amino acid synthesis, and their traits are relatively stable but vary depending on the diet. Microorganisms are also present in the gut of larvae and play a role in polystyrene biodegradation. They are tiny living organisms that can play a significant role in breaking down plastic pollution, including the breakdown of nanoplastic particles, and are being studied as a potential solution to this environmental issue. Overall, microorganisms are living organisms that play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics in the environment, and their ability to degrade synthetic plastics makes them a vital area of study in the field of environmental science.</data>
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
    </node>
