    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHOBICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrophobicity is a measure of the ability of microplastics to repel water, which affects their biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a measure of the water-repelling nature of a material, such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property of materials, including plastics like HDPE, that affects their ability to be colonized by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property of microorganisms that affects their ability to interact with and degrade plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property that affects the ability of bacterial cells to adhere to hydrophobic surfaces, such as plastic beads."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity refers to the tendency of a molecule to repel water, which can affect its biodegradability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a property of PET polymers that affects their degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property of the supernatant that affects its interaction with PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is the property of a substance to repel water, and it plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is the property of a surface to repel water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a property of surfaces that affects the degradation of Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property that refers to the ability of a substance to repel water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a property of polyethylene polymers that decreases after biodegradation and depolymerization, resulting in an increase in hydrophilicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property of polymer surfaces that affects the activity of polymer-degrading enzymes and the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a property of microplastics that changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hydrophobicity of microplastics is affected by surface oxidation and aging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property of some bacteria, such as Acinetobacter species, that contributes to their ability to adhere to polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
    </node>
