    <node id="&quot;EARTHWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Earthworms are a type of annelid that has been found to be capable of biofragmenting plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of worm that can be affected by microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of microorganism that can ingest microplastics and accumulate them in their bodies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of microorganism that has been studied for its mortality and weight reduction when exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms that can be affected by microplastics and are used as a model organism in scientific studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of soil invertebrate that are capable of degrading organic materials, but not polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are microorganisms that are being exposed to PS MPs, causing a significant increase in ROS levels."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms that have poor or no plastic-degrading ability, and are used as a comparison to other organisms such as T. molitor and Z. atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms, such as Eisenia fetida, are microorganisms that can be affected by microplastics and other pollutants in soil."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of insect that can degrade lignocellulose and other natural polymers, and have a complex gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms have bacteria in their guts that can degrade low-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of organism that can be affected by microplastics in soils."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms that have been studied for their plastic degradation activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are a type of invertebrate that has been found to ingest microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms that can break down bioplastics, such as polyactic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms that have a poor plastic-degrading ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms, specifically Eisenia andrei, can generate NPs from PE MPs in their hindgut, damaging spermatogenesis and coelomocyte viability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms, such as Eisenia andrei, are capable of biodegrading microplastics, resulting in the formation of smaller microplastics and potentially nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are microorganisms that are capable of breaking down certain types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are organisms that can be affected by microplastics and atrazine."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406</data>
    </node>
