    <node id="&quot;LAND SNAILS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Land snails are a type of insect that can degrade lignocellulose and other natural polymers, and have a complex gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails are a type of mollusk that can be affected by microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails are animals that can degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails, such as Achatina fulica, can biodegrade expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails are microorganisms that have high plastic-degrading capacities and are used to test the biodegradation of commercial plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails, such as Achatina fulica, are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, resulting in the formation of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reactive oxygen species are a group of chemicals that are being studied in the research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are a type of molecule that can cause oxidative stress, studied in the context of insect health status and the effects of polystyrene foam on the insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are highly reactive molecules that can cause oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are produced in mealworm larvae during the biodegradation of polyethylene, contributing to oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are highly reactive molecules that can cause oxidative stress, and they are influenced by the ability of microorganisms to produce enzymes that break down lipid hydroperoxides."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are chemical compounds that can trigger oxidative degradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are a type of molecule that is involved in oxidative stress and is produced during the biodegradation of microplastics by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIOXIDANT ENZYME ACTIVITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antioxidant Enzyme Activities are a biological process that helps to alleviate oxidative stress in macroinvertebrates exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzyme activities are a type of biological process that is being investigated in Tenebrio molitor after ingestion of PE microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzyme activities refer to the body's ability to detoxify reactive oxygen species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzyme activities, including SOD, GST, and CAD, are increased in mealworm larvae during the biodegradation of polyethylene, helping to alleviate oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzyme activities are measures of an organism's ability to counteract oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzyme activities are a type of biological process that is involved in the response to oxidative stress and is affected by the biodegradation of microplastics by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIPID PEROXIDATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lipid Peroxidation is a biological process that can cause cellular damage in macroinvertebrates exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a biological process that occurs when ROS react with lipids, leading to the formation of MDA and other toxic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a biological process that occurs when lipids are damaged by free radicals, leading to the generation of toxic metabolites."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a process by which the mealworm larvae's lipids are damaged by reactive oxygen species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a type of biological process that is being investigated in Tenebrio molitor after ingestion of PE microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is induced in mealworm larvae by the biodegradation of polyethylene, indicating damage to the larvae's cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation refers to the damage caused to lipids by reactive oxygen species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a biological process that occurs when free radicals attack cellular structures and biomacromolecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a measure of oxidative damage to lipids in an organism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a measure of oxidative damage to lipids, and its levels are being evaluated in response to microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a biological process that occurs in Tenebrio molitor larvae when they biodegrade microplastics, resulting in the production of malondialdehyde (MDA)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a biological process that occurs in mealworms when ingesting microplastics, leading to oxidative damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a biological process that occurs in mealworms when they are exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid peroxidation is a type of biological process that is involved in the response to oxidative stress and is affected by the biodegradation of microplastics by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physiological responses in Tenebrio molitor larvae are affected by the biodegradation of microplastics, including PE, PS, and PVC, resulting in oxidative stress and negative physiological responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Physiological responses refer to the body's reactions to internal or external stimuli, such as the ingestion of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The physiological responses are used to assess the effects of the different diets on the insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Physiological responses refer to the changes in an organism's bodily processes in response to microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Physiological responses refer to the physical and biological changes that occur in an organism in response to a stimulus or stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT LOSS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the amount of plastic degraded by microorganisms, often used to assess the effectiveness of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a health measure that can be affected by the ingestion of microplastics in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the amount of material lost during the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physical property that measures the decrease in mass of a substance, such as LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads, due to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physical property that measures the degradation of LDPE, HDPE, and PS by PM S1."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physiological response that can occur in mealworm larvae that ingest microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is the measure of the amount of material that is lost during the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physiological response of the T. molitor larvae to starvation or lack of nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a type of biological measure that is used to evaluate the biodegradation of HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the decrease in mass of polystyrene (PS) samples after incubation with strain YT2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the degradation of Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the amount of PS film degraded by bacterial cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the amount of mass lost by a material, such as polystyrene, during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss refers to the decrease in mass of mask materials during biodegradation, which is measured using DSC-TGA."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a biological measure used to calculate the degradation of PE film."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physiological response of mealworms to consuming plastic, which may be due to the lack of nutrition in the plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physiological response of the larvae to starvation or lack of nutrition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the reduction in weight of the mealworms during the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a measure of the amount of polystyrene (PS) that is degraded by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss is a physiological response that occurs in mealworms when they are fed non-biodegradable materials, such as PVC and PS, which do not provide sufficient energy for their growth and development."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS REDUCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biomass reduction is a physiological response that can occur in mealworm larvae that ingest microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL RATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Survival rates (SRs) are a biological response that is being used to study the effects of plastic degradation on microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates is a health measure of the percentage of T. molitor larvae that survive during the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates is a health measure that can be affected by plastic consumption in superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates refer to the percentage of mealworm larvae that survive during the biodegradation of PE microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates refer to the percentage of organisms that survive a particular condition, such as the ingestion of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates refer to the percentage of T. molitor larvae that survive after ingesting PS foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates are used to assess the effect of plastic diets on the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates is a health measure used to analyze the effect of polyethylene (HDPE) waste on the lesser waxworm (Achroia grisella)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates is a measure of the number of mealworms that survive."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates refer to the percentage of yellow mealworms that survive during the study, used as a measure of their tolerance to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rates of the yellow mealworms' larvae are influenced by their diet, including the consumption of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nanoplastics are a type of toxin that can have adverse effects on the environment and human health, and their environmental fate, toxicity, and risk management strategies are being researched."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are small plastic particles that are not generated and accumulated in the gut microenvironments of Tenebrio molitor larvae during the biodegradation of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics refer to extremely small plastic particles that are less than 1 micrometer in size."&lt;SEP&gt;Nanoplastics are a group of extremely small plastic particles that are less than 1 micrometer in size, equivalent to 0.001 millimeters. They can be formed through the degradation of larger plastic particles, such as polyethylene and polyethylene terephthalate (PET), during biodegradation processes. Nanoplastics are considered emerging environmental contaminants and have been found to pose significant environmental problems due to their high mobility and increased toxicity. They can be ingested by organisms, causing harm and leading to impairment of physiological function and metabolism in both invertebrates and vertebrates. Nanoplastics have been detected in aquatic environments, where they can cause toxic effects and harm the ecosystem. Despite their small size, nanoplastics are a type of plastic pollution that can have harmful effects on the environment and human health. Research has shown that nanoplastics can be biodegraded by microorganisms, but their accumulation is not always observed in certain organisms, such as mealworm larvae. Overall, nanoplastics are a key focus of study due to their potential environmental impacts, and their effects on living organisms and the ecosystem are being closely examined.&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are small plastic particles that can be found in the environment and can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are very small plastic particles that can be ingested by mealworms and affect their gut microbiome and metabolome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO-DIET BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Co-Diet Bran is a nutrient that can help to alleviate oxidative stress in Tenebrio molitor fed with polylactic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet bran is a supplement that can be added to the diet of mealworm larvae to enhance their biomass levels and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet bran is a supplement that enhances the PP consumption rate of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTESTINAL TRACTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intestinal tracts refer to the digestive system of organisms, such as mealworm larvae, where microplastics can accumulate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic refers to a type of material that is being studied for its degradation and pollution in the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;PLASTIC is a general term for a broad category of synthetic materials, including Polyethylene, Polystyrene, Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET), Polypropylene (PP), Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC), High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE), Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE), Ethylene-Vinyl Acetate (EVA), and Styrofoam. These materials can cause biochemical perturbations in insects and have varying effects on insect metabolism. PLASTIC is being used as a substrate to feed mealworms, such as Tenebrio molitor, with its presence affecting the fatty acid degradation pathway and potentially being toxic to the mealworms. However, certain microorganisms, including bacteria like Klebsiella oxytoca, and the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor, have been found to biodegrade PLASTIC, breaking down its synthetic polymers. The biodegradation of PLASTIC is an important process in the environment, and different types of PLASTIC, such as polystyrene and polyethylene, can be biodegraded by macro-organisms and microbial strains. Despite being widely used, PLASTIC can be difficult to degrade in natural environments, but research has shown that mealworms and their gut microbiome can consume and biodegrade various types of PLASTIC, leading to changes in molecular weight and potential for biodegradation. Overall, PLASTIC is a complex and multifaceted material that requires further study to understand its effects on the environment and its potential for biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d7f6907ee7ce47b00537b1458fcc029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939800f545beed77f5a7d1c026c250fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROPLASTICS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE microplastics are a type of microplastic that can induce oxidative stress in Tenebrio larvae and other invertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics are a type of plastic that is being used as a feedstock for Tenebrio molitor and are being biodegraded by the insect."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics are the primary substance being fed to the mealworm larvae for biodegradation, and their structure and properties affect digestibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics are the small plastic particles being degraded by the mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics are small plastic particles that can be harmful to insects and other organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics refer to small plastic particles made of polyethylene, which are being degraded by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics are a type of microplastic that undergoes biodegradation by T. molitor larvae, resulting in the formation of long-chain alkanes and oxygen-containing compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics are a type of plastic that is ingested by mealworm larvae, affecting their growth and survival."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ROS are molecules being measured in the mealworm larvae to assess their physiological response to plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are a type of metabolite that is being investigated in Tenebrio molitor after ingestion of PE microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which play a crucial role in cell signaling and homeostasis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are metabolites involved in oxidative stress, and their measurement is used to determine the level of oxidative stress in cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are molecules that can cause oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) are highly reactive molecules that can cause oxidative stress in macroinvertebrates exposed to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are molecules that can cause oxidative stress in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) are highly reactive molecules that can contribute to the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS is a biological marker that was analyzed in mealworms to identify oxidative stress and damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) is a biological marker that indicates oxidative stress and damage in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are a biological marker of oxidative stress in Tenebrio molitor larvae, indicating the level of oxidative damage caused by microplastic biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFRAGMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biofragmentation is a biological process that involves the breakdown of complex molecules into simpler fragments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is a type of biological process that is being used to describe the breakdown of plastics by Tenebrio molitor and other organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation refers to the breakdown of plastic particles into smaller components using biological processes, such as biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which microplastics are broken down into smaller pieces by an organism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which microplastics are broken down into smaller pieces by biological organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which organisms break down plastics into smaller pieces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which plastics are broken down into smaller particles, such as microplastics and nanoparticles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which organisms, such as mealworms, break down larger plastics into smaller particles, including microplastics and potentially nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which plastic particles are broken down into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the process by which microplastics are broken down into smaller particles through biological means, such as digestion by insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEPIDOPTERA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORDER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that have been observed taking plastics as a food supply and can decompose them in vivo."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes moths and butterflies, some of which have larvae that can degrade nanoplastic particles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes moths capable of biodegrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes the species Pyralidae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes the Greater Wax Moth (WM)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes species that feed on EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes species capable of degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes moths and butterflies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lepidoptera is an order of insects that includes species that have been observed taking plastics as a food supply."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PYRALIDAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pyralidae is a family of insects that includes moths, with a range of species and characteristics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pyralidae is a family of insects that includes species capable of biodegrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pyralidae is a family of moths that includes some species capable of biodegrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pyralidae is a family of moths that includes species capable of biodegrading microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pyralidae is a family of insects that includes species such as G. mellonella, which can biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pyralidae is a family of moths that includes species that chew and ingest plastic package materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTARCTIC KRILL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antarctic krill are organisms that have a poor plastic-degrading ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antarctic krill are organisms that have poor or no plastic-degrading ability, and are used as a comparison to other organisms such as T. molitor and Z. atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antarctic krill is a species of crustacean that has been found to be capable of biofragmenting plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antarctic krill is a type of crustacean that lacks plastic-degrading ability and is often used as a model organism in studies on plastic fragmentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antarctic krill, such as Euphausia superba, are capable of ingesting and biodegrading microplastics, resulting in the formation of smaller microplastics and potentially nanoplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MNP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MNP is a type of metabolite that is being produced during the biofragmentation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZHONGLIAN PETROCHEMICAL CO.&quot;">
