    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION PROCESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradation process is the process by which microorganisms break down organic materials, including plastics, into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;The BIODEGRADATION PROCESS is a biological process by which microorganisms, including insects such as Tenebrio molitor larvae and bacteria, break down organic materials, including plastics like polyethylene, polystyrene, polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polypropylene, into simpler compounds. This process involves a series of biological reactions and steps that occur within the gut microbiome of certain organisms, such as the mealworm larvae, and results in the depolymerization and mineralization of complex substances. The BIODEGRADATION PROCESS is a natural mechanism by which living organisms, including microorganisms, contribute to the decomposition of organic matter, including plastics, and plays a crucial role in the ecosystem. It is a complex process that involves the breakdown of organic substances into smaller components, ultimately leading to the formation of simpler compounds. The BIODEGRADATION PROCESS is influenced by various factors, including the molecular weight of the polymer, surface hydrophobicity, molecular weight distribution, and crystallinity, and involves the activation of innate digestive enzymes, oxidases, and other functions. The process can break down a wide range of organic materials, including polyethylene polymers, PET, polystyrene, polyurethane foam, and other plastics, into smaller molecules, resulting in a decrease in molecular weight and an increase in hydrophilicity. The BIODEGRADATION PROCESS of polystyrene (PS) polymers by mealworms involves the alteration of intestinal metabolic pathways and the interaction with the gut microbiome. Additionally, other organisms, such as Z. atratus, facilitate the BIODEGRADATION PROCESS, which can break down microplastics into smaller molecules through a series of chemical and biological reactions. Overall, the BIODEGRADATION PROCESS is a vital biological process that involves the breakdown of organic materials by microorganisms, and it plays a significant role in maintaining the balance of the ecosystem. The BIODEGRADATION PROCESS is a potential solution to the problem of plastic waste, and its understanding is crucial for the development of strategies to mitigate the impact of plastic pollution on the environment.&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation process is the series of steps that occur when an organism breaks down a substance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation process refers to the process by which microorganisms break down organic matter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestion is a biological process being studied in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is a biological process by which mealworms break down and assimilate the nutrients from the plastics they consume."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is a biological process that the T. molitor larvae use to break down nutrients, including PET microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is the process by which insects break down and degrade Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is the process by which the mealworm larvae break down the PE microplastics and bran into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is the process by which mealworms break down polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is a biological process that can be used to mitigate plastics present in food waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which organisms break down and utilize nutrients, which may be affected by the degradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is a biological process that involves the breakdown of nutrients in the gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is the process by which organisms break down food into nutrients, aided by gut bacteria in the case of the velvetbean caterpillar."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is a biological process by which mealworms break down and mineralize plastics, involving the enhancement of plastic-degrading microbial activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is a biological process by which microorganisms break down plastic particles into smaller fragments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion is the process by which organisms break down plastic particles into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestion refers to the process by which mealworms break down and extract nutrients from PS MPs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT UPTAKE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrient uptake is a biological process carried out by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrient uptake refers to the process by which the mealworm larvae absorb and utilize the nutrients from the PE microplastics and bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrient uptake refers to the process by which mealworms absorb nutrients from their food."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrient uptake refers to the process by which an organism, such as the T. molitor larvae, absorbs and utilizes nutrients from its environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTESTINAL BLOCKAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intestinal blockage is a physiological response that can occur in organisms that ingest microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intestinal blockage is a physiological response that can occur when organisms ingest microplastics, leading to physical damage and decreased nutrient uptake."&lt;SEP&gt;"Intestinal blockage is a potential physiological response of the mealworm larvae to the ingestion of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSICAL DAMAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physical damage is a physiological response that can occur when organisms ingest microplastics, leading to intestinal blockage and decreased nutrient uptake."&lt;SEP&gt;"Physical damage is a potential physiological response of the mealworm larvae to the ingestion of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrolase is an enzyme that is involved in the biodegradation of PE by the mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolase is an enzyme that is involved in the breakdown of molecules using water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolase is an enzyme that is involved in the degradation of PS and is upregulated in LG3 when using Mi-PS as a carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolase is an enzyme that is involved in the degradation of PS under anaerobic conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidase is a test used to biochemically characterize the bacterial isolates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme that can be produced by certain bacteria, including some species of Bacillus and Stenotrophomonas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme that is involved in the biodegradation of PE by the mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme with activity levels measured in different variants, indicating its role in certain biological processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme that is produced by some of the bacterial isolates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme that is involved in the degradation of PS but is not upregulated in LG3 when using Mi-PS as a carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidase is an enzyme with activity levels measured in different variants, indicating its role in certain biological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROS (REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ROS is a type of reactive molecule that is produced during the biodegradation of PE and causes oxidative stress in the mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MDA (MALONDIALDEHYDE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MDA is a molecule that is produced during lipid peroxidation and is used as a marker of oxidative damage in the mealworm larvae."</data>
