    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CELLULOSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cellulose is a biological marker that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of biodegradable material that can be broken down by certain microorganisms, including those found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;Cellulose is a biological concept that refers to a type of polysaccharide, a complex carbohydrate, and a biopolymer that is a key component of plant cell walls. It is a type of nutrient that can be found in plant cell walls and can be used as a food source for microorganisms. Cellulose is involved in the degradation pathway of lignocellulose and can be biodegraded by certain bacteria, microorganisms, and insects such as Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms. The degradation of cellulose is related to the activity of sugar-degrading enzymes and is an important process in the metabolism of plants and microorganisms. Cellulose is not a type of plastic, but rather a type of organic compound and a nutritional component found in feedstocks. It has been found to be biodegradable by Tenebrio molitor larvae and other microorganisms, showing its potential for degradation by microorganisms. The study of cellulose biodegradability is important for understanding its effects on the microbiome and hydrolytic enzymes in the larval digestive tract of T. molitor. Overall, Cellulose is a type of organic matter that can be broken down by microorganisms in the gut of mealworms and other insects, and its degradation is a key process in the metabolism of plants and microorganisms.&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of organic material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and other insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of biodegradable polymer that is found in plant cell walls."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of nutrient that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of organic compound that is being biodegraded by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of nutrient that can be degraded by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of nutrient that can be digested by mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cellulose is a type of nutrient that is degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae, and its degradation is related to the activity of sugar-degrading enzymes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC PROFILE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes that are present in an organism."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae is affected by the biodegradation of cellulose, polyethylene, and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae is characterized by the activity of various enzymes, including phosphatases, esterases, and sugar-degrading enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes produced by an organism and can play a role in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes present in an organism or community, which can be used to biodegrade various substances, including plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the types and amounts of enzymes present in an organism, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes produced by microorganisms, which are involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes present in an organism, and has been found to change in Tenebrio molitor when they biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymatic profile refers to the set of enzymes that are produced by an organism and are involved in various biological processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile of Tenebrio molitor larvae is characterized by the activity of various enzymes, including phosphatases, esterases, and sugar-degrading enzymes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Proteobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that plays a crucial role in various ecosystems, including the gut microbiome of mealworms, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae and yellow mealworms. This phylum is composed of several classes, including Alphaproteobacteria, Betaproteobacteria, and Gammaproteobacteria, and is involved in numerous functions, including nitrogen fixation, decomposition of organic matter, and biodegradation of plastics and other xenobiotic pollutants, such as PVC, LDPE, and polystyrene (PS). Proteobacteria is a dominant phylum in the gut bacterial communities of yellow mealworms fed with different diets, including PU- and EVA-fed groups, as well as in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed with medium and high molecular weight polystyrene. It is also a dominant phylum in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants. Proteobacteria has been found to be more abundant in PS samples and is a major component of the microbial community involved in degrading polystyrene. Additionally, Proteobacteria is present in the gut microbial community of larvae fed with polyethylene terephthalate (PET), although to a lesser extent than Firmicutes. The abundance of Proteobacteria can be affected by different feeding regimes, with decreased abundance observed in mealworms fed with PS and cellulose acetate (CS). Nevertheless, Proteobacteria remains a significant portion of the bacterial community in mealworms, contributing to their ability to degrade various types of waste and pollutants. Proteobacteria has been found to have an inverse relationship with Firmicutes in mealworms fed with certain diets. Furthermore, Proteobacteria is a type of bacteria that was predominantly found in the T. molitor larval gut isolates and is present in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, with a relative frequency of 76% in the PS group and 57% in the control (CT) group. Overall, Proteobacteria is a vital phylum that is widely present in the gut microbiome of mealworms and other organisms, and its functions and interactions with other microorganisms are essential for understanding its role in ecosystems.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTEROIDES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteroides is a bacterium that can be found in the gut microbiome of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a class of bacteria that is part of the dominant phyla in the gut microbiome of mealworms, contributing to the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a dominant class in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a genus of bacteria found in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a genus of bacteria that belongs to the order Bacteroidales."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a phylum of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, and it contributes to their ability to degrade various types of waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a dominant phylum in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor, involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a type of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of mealworms, making up a small portion of the bacterial community."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a type of bacteria that can be found in the gut microbiota of certain insects, including termites."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a dominant class in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a genus of bacteria found in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a genus of bacteria that belongs to the order Bacteroidales."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a phylum of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, and it contributes to their ability to degrade various types of waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FIRMICUTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria that plays a significant role in the gut microbiome of various organisms, including mealworms and Tenebrio molitor larvae. It is a dominant phylum in the microbial communities of these organisms, with varying proportions across different feeding variants. Firmicutes includes several classes, such as Bacilli, and is represented in both the bran-fed and EPS-fed groups. This phylum is also composed of various orders, including Sphingobacteriales, and genera, such as Terrimonas, Lactococcus, Pediococcus, and Paraclostridium. Firmicutes is known to contribute to the biodegradation of plastics and waste, and its abundance is affected by the diet of the host organism. For instance, Firmicutes is decreased in relative abundance in mealworms fed with PS-only or PS plus bran diets, but is increased in abundance in the gut of mealworms fed PS and CS. Additionally, Firmicutes has a higher relative abundance in PS and CS groups compared to the CK group. This phylum is also found to be dominant in the gut microbial community of larvae fed with PET, and its presence is crucial for the degradation of various types of waste. Firmicutes is also present in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, with a relative frequency of 13% in the PS group and 8% in the CT group. Furthermore, Firmicutes is a phylum to which some PS-degrading bacteria belong, and it is predominant in the guts of 20 d PS-fed groups. Overall, Firmicutes is a diverse and important phylum of bacteria that plays a vital role in the gut microbiome of various organisms, including insects, and is involved in various ecological processes, such as the degradation of plastics and waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTINOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Actinobacteria is a class of bacteria that includes the order Actinomycetales and the genus Arthrobacter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinobacteria is a dominant phylum in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that includes many species that are found in the guts of insects."&lt;SEP&gt;Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that plays a crucial role in various ecological processes. Actinobacteria is found in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms, and is involved in the biodegradation of plastics, decomposition of organic matter, and production of antibiotics. This phylum is a dominant group in the microbial communities of mealworm larvae, with varying proportions across different feeding variants. Actinobacteria includes species with the alkB gene, styrene-degrading abilities, and PS-degrading species, contributing to the degradation of various types of waste, including HDPE. Actinobacteria is also associated with nitrogen metabolism and fixation functions in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio species. The abundance of Actinobacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms is affected by the diet, and it is part of the dominant phyla in the gut microbiome of these organisms. Actinobacteria includes genera such as Arthrobacter and species like Kocuria sp., which are overrepresented in certain environments. Additionally, Actinobacteria is present in the gut of other insects, such as Alphitobius diaperinus, with varying relative frequencies. Overall, Actinobacteria is a diverse and ecologically important group of bacteria that contributes to various biodegradation processes and is an essential component of the gut microbiome of several insect species.&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that is involved in various functions, including the production of amino acids and decomposition of organic matter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinobacteria is a phylum of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, and it contributes to their ability to degrade various types of waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6</data>
