      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Esterases are a type of enzyme that breaks down ester bonds and is involved in the degradation of polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are a type of enzyme that is involved in the degradation of polyethylene waste by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that are involved in the degradation of polystyrene and are found in the digestive tract of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that have been found to degrade polystyrene, indicating their potential role in the degradation of this product."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes involved in the biodegradation of plastics, acting on the breakdown of ester bonds in plastic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics, acting on ester bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are a type of enzyme that is involved in the degradation of polyurethane, particularly in the hydrolysis of PU bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that break specific bonds in polyurethane molecules and were measured in the gut of the mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are a type of enzyme that breaks down esters, a type of chemical bond."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are a type of extracellular enzyme produced by bacterial cells, capable of breaking down esters."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes produced by Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9, involved in the degradation of environmental pollutants such as di(2-ethylhexyl) phthalate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that are involved in the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are a type of enzyme that is involved in the degradation of polyethylene waste by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that are involved in the decomposition of polystyrene."</data>
