    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lactococcus is a genus of bacteria that is strongly associated with the biodegradation of low molecular weight polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;Lactococcus is a genus of bacteria that is commonly found in the gut microbiome of various insects, including Tenebrio molitor larvae, superworms, and yellow mealworms. This genus of bacteria is known to be involved in the degradation of plastic waste, including polyurethane (PU), polystyrene (PS), and polypropylene (PP). Lactococcus is a dominant genus in the gut microbiome of insects fed on plastic diets, such as PE and PS, and is also associated with the biodegradation of styrofoam. As a type of lactic acid bacteria, Lactococcus contributes to the health of the gut microbiome environment and plays a role in regulating and maintaining the health of the gut microbiome. Lactococcus is abundant in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor and mealworms, and its relative abundance has been found to increase in the gut of yellow mealworms after feeding on masks. Overall, Lactococcus is a significant genus of bacteria that is involved in the biodegradation of plastics and contributes to the health of the gut microbiome of insects.&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus is a type of bacteria that can degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus is a type of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae and is involved in the degradation of plastic waste."</data>
