    <node id="&quot;GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut bacteria are microorganisms present in the gut of mealworms, playing a role in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the digestive tract of Achroia grisella larvae and might be responsible for their ability to degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria, such as Spiroplasma, Weissella, and Lactococcus, play a crucial role in the biodegradation of styrofoam by the mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;Gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the gut of various insects, including mealworms, superworms, and Tenebrio molitor. They play a crucial role in the degradation of various compounds, such as polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and expanded polystyrene (EPS). The gut bacteria are involved in the depolymerization and mineralization of these plastics, with their activity affected by antibiotics such as gentamicin. They are also capable of decomposing lignocelluloses and contribute to the digestion and development of the insects. The gut bacteria have been found to include species such as Bacillus, Stenotrophomonas, Pantoea, and Erwinia. They are responsible for biodegrading plastics into carbon dioxide and biomass, and their colonies can be counted and studied over time. The gut bacteria have a symbiotic relationship with the insects, contributing to their ability to adapt to different foods and environments. Overall, gut bacteria play an essential role in the biodegradation process of various plastics and compounds, and their study can provide valuable insights into the development of new biodegradation methods.&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the gut of the larvae and play a crucial role in the biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the gut of yellow mealworms and play a role in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the gut of T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae and play a role in LDPE depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are part of the gut microbiome of mealworms and play a crucial role in the biodegradation of PS polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria play an important role in mealworms' ability to adapt to different foods and are capable of decomposing lignocelluloses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut bacteria are microorganisms that live in the gut of insects, such as mealworms, and play a role in the degradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca6acde6809ed3c727d7bffdbfd1ada&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
