    <node id="&quot;EXTRACELLULAR ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Extracellular enzymes are biological molecules that might be involved in the degradation of polyethylene by Achroia grisella larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are enzymes that are produced by microorganisms and can break down polyurethane (PU) outside of the cell."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are enzymes that are produced by microorganisms in the gut microbiome of mealworms and can break down PU."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are secreted by gut microbiota and catalyze the decomposition of polystyrene into fragments with a low molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are the enzymes that are excreted by microbes to catalyze the depolymerization of Styrofoam fragments into small-molecule products."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are the enzymes that are excreted by the gut microbiota to catalyze the depolymerization of Styrofoam fragments into small-molecule products."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes, such as lipases, esterases, proteases, and laccases, play a crucial role in the degradation of polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are produced by microorganisms in the gut of yellow mealworms and play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes are secreted by gut microbiota and catalyze the decomposition of polystyrene into fragments with a low molecular weight."</data>
