    <node id="&quot;BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria are a type of microorganism that are found in the gut and play a role in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Bacteria are a type of microorganism that can be found in a variety of environments and play a crucial role in the biodegradation of various materials, including plastics. They are capable of breaking down different types of plastics, such as polyethylene, polystyrene, high-density polyethylene (HDPE), bioplastics, cellulose, and EPS. Bacteria are being studied for their potential to degrade plastics, including nanoplastics, and their ability to metabolize and degrade various plastic compounds. They can be isolated and cultured from the gut of certain insects, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, also known as mealworms, and Alphitobius diaperinus, where they contribute to the degradation of plastics like polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polystyrene. Certain strains of bacteria, like Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2, are able to break down specific types of plastics. Bacteria have the ability to adhere to surfaces, with their adhesion capacity directly proportional to the number of flagella. They are significant microorganisms that are involved in the biodegradation of plastic compounds and are being researched for their potential to help mitigate plastic pollution. Additionally, bacteria can be extracted from earthworms' gut for soil restoration, highlighting their importance in various biological processes. Overall, bacteria are a key component in the degradation of plastics and are being explored for their potential to address the issue of plastic pollution.&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are the microorganisms used in the experiment to degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria are microorganisms that are isolated from the digestive tracts of mealworm larvae, with a total of 98102 reads representing 972 species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001</data>
