    <node id="&quot;CANNIBALISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cannibalism is a behavior exhibited by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae, where they consume each other."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed in mealworm larvae, regardless of the diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a process that may occur in the guts of insect larvae, which can affect the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a biological concept that refers to the behavior of mealworms eating each other when they are not fed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed in mealworms, where adults consume larvae, which can decrease the utilization of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed in the mealworm larvae, where they eat each other."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a biological process that occurs among the mealworm insects, affecting the overall consumption rate of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism refers to the behavior of mealworms eating each other, which can affect the efficiency of polyurethane degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavioral response observed in Tenebrio molitor larvae when they are fed with different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism refers to the behavior of mealworms eating other mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a biological behavior that occurs in some organisms, including lesser waxworms, in which they consume other members of their own species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed in mealworms, possibly caused by hunger and influenced by diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior where larvae eat other larvae, which can occur when there is a lack of food or nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior exhibited by Z. atratus larvae under nutrition limitation conditions, with a higher cannibal rate observed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior exhibited by some microorganisms, including Z. atratus larvae, in which they consume other members of their own species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a biological process that can occur in microorganisms such as T. molitor and Z. atratus, which can contribute to the biodegradation of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavioral response observed in the mealworms, particularly in the PS-fed and starved groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed in mealworm larvae, regardless of the diet."</data>
