    <node id="&quot;NITROGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrogenase is an enzyme responsible for nitrogen fixation, and its gene (nifH) is targeted in the PCR amplifications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogenase is an enzyme responsible for the nitrogen-fixing activity in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogenase is an enzyme that is involved in nitrogen fixation and is active in Pantoee sp."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogenase is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in the fixation of atmospheric nitrogen, and its activity is dependent on the availability of trace elements such as iron, sulfur, vanadium, and manganese."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogenase is an enzyme that is responsible for fixing nitrogen in the atmosphere."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTERASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Esterase is an enzyme produced by Pseudomonas and Bacillus, associated with high impact polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that has been detected in culturable gut bacterial flora isolated from diamondback moth, Plutella xylostella, and may play a role in indoxylcarb degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that shows increased activity in larvae fed polyethylene diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that hydrolyzes p-nitrophenyl acetate, and its activity is determined spectrophotometrically."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that has been found to be involved in the degradation of di(2-ethylhexyl)phthalate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that is produced by some of the bacterial isolates, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa M2 and Achromobacter xylosidans M9."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that has been found to play a role in the biodegradation of plastics, including polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterase is an enzyme that can degrade certain types of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </node>
