    <node id="&quot;LIGNOCELLULOSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lignocellulose is a biological concept that refers to a type of complex organic material that is degraded by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a complex organic material that can be degraded by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a complex organic material that can be degraded by various microorganisms, relevant to the study of insect microbiosa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a component of the cardboard diet, influencing the growth and development of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a nutrient that is deficient in nitrogen compounds and is the basis of the termite diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of carbohydrate that is found in plant cell walls and can be used as a food source for microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of nutrient that can be degraded by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of nutrient used as a diet for insects, resulting in a low number of dead insects and a high number of larvae, but also a severe loss of body mass at the last measurement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of organic compound found in plant cell walls, which can be consumed and digested by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a complex organic material that is degraded by mealworms through a series of metabolic pathways."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a natural polymer that can be degraded by certain enzymes and microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of complex carbohydrate found in plant cell walls."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of natural polymer found in plant cell walls, and can be degraded by certain insects and microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a type of nutritional component found in the feedstocks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose was measured in the CB and CS feedstocks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignocellulose is a component of the natural diet of T. molitor larvae and may be related to their ability to degrade PET."</data>
