    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN FIXATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">NITROGEN FIXATION is a biological process by which certain microorganisms, such as nitrogen-fixing bacteria, convert atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form that can be utilized by living organisms, including plants, animals, and insects, such as Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms. This process involves the conversion of nitrogen gas into a form that can be used by organisms, such as ammonia or nitrate, and is crucial for the survival of certain organisms, like mealworms, when they are fed a nitrogen-deficient diet, including low-nutrient substances like PET, or vinyl plastics. NITROGEN FIXATION occurs in various environments, including the gut microbiome of larvae, where microorganisms provide a source of nitrogen to the larvae, allowing them to overcome nitrogen limitation. This process is essential for the survival of certain organisms and plays a vital role in the ecosystem, as it allows microorganisms to convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form that can be used by other living organisms, thereby facilitating the circulation of nitrogen in the environment. Overall, NITROGEN FIXATION is a vital biological process that enables the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form, supporting the growth and development of various organisms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic degradation is a biological process by which certain microorganisms can break down certain types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;PLASTIC DEGRADATION is a biological process by which microorganisms, such as those present in the gut of Alphitobius diaperinus, and other organisms like yellow mealworms and superworms, break down plastics into smaller molecules, resulting in a reduction in molecular weight and chemical modifications. This process involves the breakdown of plastics by microorganisms, such as Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus, as well as enzymes, and can be facilitated by various organisms, including insects. PLASTIC DEGRADATION can break down a wide range of plastics, including polyethylene, microplastics, polypropylene, LDPE, LLDPE, HDPE, PE, PS, and PVC, into simpler compounds or smaller components, such as CO2. The process of PLASTIC DEGRADATION is being studied in the context of mealworms and their gut microbiome, and it is an important process that is being investigated to understand how microorganisms and other organisms can help to break down and mitigate the impact of plastic waste on the environment. PLASTIC DEGRADATION can be used to detoxify the environment and can result in the conversion of plastics into smaller, more manageable components that can be further degraded or utilized. Overall, PLASTIC DEGRADATION is a complex process that involves the breakdown of plastics by microorganisms and other organisms, and it has the potential to play a significant role in reducing the amount of plastic waste in the environment.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-866df96ed7f051acf60ebd0e82d9a19e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
