    <node id="&quot;BIOFILM FORMATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biofilm formation is a biological process by which microorganisms attach to and grow on surfaces, including plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a biological process that occurs when microorganisms attach to a surface and form a complex community, which is relevant to the biodegradation of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process by which microorganisms like Rhodococcus ruber and Microoccus luteus adhere to surfaces and degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which PM S1 colonizes the surface of LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process by which bacteria attach to surfaces and form complex communities, which can contribute to PE degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which microorganisms form a community on a surface."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which microorganisms adhere to surfaces and form complex communities, which can enhance their ability to degrade certain materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which microorganisms form complex communities on surfaces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process by which bacteria, like Rhodococcus ruber, adhere to surfaces and develop complex communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which microorganisms, such as Rhodococcus ruber, form complex communities on surfaces."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation refers to the process by which microorganisms form complex communities on surfaces, including plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a biological process by which microorganisms attach to surfaces and form complex communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process by which microorganisms, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, form a complex community on a surface."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation refers to the process by which microorganisms form complex communities on surfaces, and it is influenced by the ability of the microorganisms to produce enzymes that break down organic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a biological process by which microorganisms attach to surfaces and form complex communities, which can initiate biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116</data>
