    <node id="&quot;KLEBSIELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Klebsiella is a bacterium associated with PS diet in mealworms, indicating its possible involvement in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that is associated with the degradation of polyethylene and polypropylene in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a bacterium that can be found in the gut microbiome of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a type of bacteria that does not support gut health and is found in higher presence in certain diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that has been isolated from Tenebrio molitor and has shown biodegradable activity against bioplastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a type of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that WJ2020 is 99% similar to, based on 16S rRNA sequencing results."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that includes the strain WJ2020, which is capable of degrading PS film."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that has been found to be present in the gut of mealworms and has nitrogen-fixing abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that has been identified as an important nitrogen-fixing symbiont within the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that includes many nitrogen-fixing species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria that is found to be an important nitrogen-fixing symbiont of Styrofoam-eating mealworms, with high nitrogen fixation rates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria"&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterium associated with PP diet in superworms, suggesting its potential role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that is associated with the degradation of polypropylene in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterium that has been reported to have styrene-degrading abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes species capable of degrading styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that is abundant in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor and may be involved in biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a type of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that is decreased in abundance in the gut microbiota of mealworms fed with PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus that is predominant in the larval gut microbiota of all diet groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes species like Enterobacter hormaechei, which is capable of biodegrading polystyrene microplastics under anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that is present in the posterior gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that has been identified in the gut of mealworms and plays a role in the biodegradation of styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a type of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of greater wax moth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a type of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes species capable of degrading plastics, such as Enterobacter hormaechei LG3, which can degrade PS under both anaerobic and aerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes species capable of degrading polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes species such as Enterobacter ludwigii, which protects against DDS-induced colitis through choline-mediated immune tolerance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes strains with potential for PS biodegradation, such as E. hormaechei LG3."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that has been found to have nitrogen-fixing abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria that includes many nitrogen-fixing species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KLUYVERA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kluyvera is a bacterium associated with PP diet in mealworms, implying its involvement in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a genus of bacteria that is associated with the degradation of polypropylene in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a genus that is predominant in the larval gut microbiota of all diet groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a type of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of superworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a type of bacteria that has been found to be predominant in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a genus of bacteria that has been found to be present in the gut of mealworms and has nitrogen-fixing abilities."</data>
