    <node id="&quot;ASPERGILLUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aspergillus is a fungus involved in PS plastic degradation in mealworm guts, suggesting its potential role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a fungus that has been reported to degrade PE plastic and is considered a potential candidate for LDPE degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi, with species versicolor, which belongs to the family Trichocomaceae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that exhibits high abundance in the PS- and CS-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that can mediate adherence to host constituents and conceal hyphal β-glucan from the immune system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that has been confirmed to have plastic biodegradation capacity and appeared in all the mask diets with a high relative abundance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that is found in the gut of yellow mealworms and is associated with the degradation of plastics such as PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that appeared to be overrepresented in PS samples compared to CT samples, with a Log2-fold change of 13.9."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that includes species capable of degrading various plastic materials, such as polyethylene and polystyrene, which may indicate a potential role in plastic breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that is significantly over-represented in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a type of fungus that has been found in association with plastic compounds and has been proposed to be capable of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a type of fungus that has been reported to have plastics degradation ability and is present in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a type of microorganism that is found in the gut of yellow mealworms and is associated with the biodegradation of PU plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi that has been found to be capable of biodegrading thermally treated low-density polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d40b7debb6497167fa1aec874ebce45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
