    <node id="&quot;TRICHODERMA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trichoderma is a fungus involved in PS plastic degradation in mealworm guts, indicating its potential role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungus that has been reported to increase in abundance in PS-fed lesser mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a genus of fungi that appeared to be overrepresented in PS samples compared to CT samples, with a Log2-fold change of 11.8."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a genus of fungi that includes species capable of degrading plastics, such as LDPE, which may indicate a potential role in plastic breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a genus of fungi that is significantly over-represented in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a type of fungus that has been found in association with plastic compounds and has been proposed to be capable of plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Z. ATRATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Z. atratus is a type of microorganism that is capable of biodegrading LDPE, as mentioned in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;Z. atratus is a genus of insects, specifically a species of darkling beetle larvae, commonly referred to as superworms. They are known for their ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), and polyurethane (PU) foam. Z. atratus has been studied for its ability to consume and degrade plastics, with some studies showing a higher consumption rate compared to other insects, such as the yellow mealworm. However, they may experience significant weight loss during this process. The biodegradation process of Z. atratus is unique and differs from other microorganisms, such as T. molitor. They have been found to exhibit both broad and limited extent depolymerization patterns when degrading polymers like PS and LDPE. Z. atratus has also been observed to exhibit cannibalism, particularly under nutrition limitation conditions. Despite this, they have been used in experiments to study plastic degradation, including the biodegradation of Styrofoam and other plastics. Their ability to degrade plastics makes them a subject of interest in the study of biodegradation and plastic pollution. Overall, Z. atratus is a species of insect larvae that plays a significant role in the biodegradation of various plastics, and further research is needed to fully understand their capabilities and potential applications.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">ATR-FTIR, or Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition and structure of various materials, including plastics such as polyethylene, polystyrene, polypropylene, and LDPE substrates. ATR-FTIR is utilized to characterize the functional groups in materials like frass samples and to examine the chemical composition of plastic materials, including nanoplastic particles. This spectroscopic technique is also employed to investigate the degradation of plastics, such as the oxidation and degradation of polypropylene, and to analyze the chemical composition of other materials like PE mulching film, HDPE, PS and PU foam, and surgical mask materials. Furthermore, ATR-FTIR is used to study the molecular structure of materials, including PS film, and to detect changes in the chemical composition of materials. Overall, ATR-FTIR is a valuable tool for analyzing the chemical properties of a wide range of materials, providing valuable insights into their composition, structure, and degradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ITS GENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ITS gene is a gene used as a marker for fungal communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The ITS gene, or internal transcribed spacer gene, is a genetic marker used to identify and analyze fungal communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"ITS gene is a genetic marker used to identify and analyze the microorganisms in the gut core microbiome of the yellow mealworms."</data>
