    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworm is a type of insect that can biodegrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are a type of microorganism that consumes plastic, with a higher consumption rate than yellow mealworms, but experiences significant weight loss."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm, also known as Zophobas atratus, is a genus of insect that can biodegrade polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is an insect that can biodegrade polystyrene and other plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is a type of larvae that can feed on PS and has a high survival rate, with a significant decrease in mass weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is a type of microorganism that has the highest ability to degrade PS into low-molecular-weight substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is one of the three species of larvae being studied, and its gut microbiome is being analyzed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yellow Mealworm is a type of microorganism that has a high degradation efficiency of PS and can destroy the benzene ring."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yellow mealworm is a type of insect that is being considered as a source of animal protein for humans in bioregenerative life support systems."&lt;SEP&gt;The Yellow Mealworm, also known as Tenebrio molitor, is an insect species that has been found to possess the ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, polypropylene, and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). This capability is attributed to the gut microbiome of the Yellow Mealworm, which responds to diet shifts and plays a crucial role in the biodegradation process. The Yellow Mealworm's larvae can ingest and participate in the degradation of polystyrene, and the insect has been studied for its ability to transform plastic, specifically PS plastic, making it suitable for the continuous transformation of plastic. Additionally, the Yellow Mealworm has been approved as a food source for humans by the EU Food Safety Agency. While the consumption rate of Yellow Mealworms is lower than that of superworms, they experience a slight increase in body weight change. The Yellow Mealworm's ability to biodegrade plastics has been extensively studied, and it is considered a valuable species for examining the optimal transformation efficiency and most suitable type of plastic for biodegradation. Furthermore, the Yellow Mealworm has been found to be capable of degrading surgical masks, highlighting its potential for environmental applications. Overall, the Yellow Mealworm, Tenebrio molitor, is a remarkable insect species with significant potential for biodegradation and environmental sustainability.&lt;SEP&gt;"Yellow mealworm is a type of larvae that can feed on PS, but has a lower survival rate compared to superworm and greater wax moth, with a moderate decrease in mass weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yellow mealworm is one of the three species of larvae being studied, and its gut microbiome is being analyzed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene Foam is a type of plastic that is commonly used for food and beverage containers and can be biodegraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of diet fed to insects, studied for its potential effects on the health status of the insects and the cytotoxic properties of the insect extracts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic consumed by superworms and yellow mealworms, with evidence of oxidation and de-polymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic that undergoes oxidation and depolymerization in the larval guts of superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic that can be degraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic waste that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor, with a mass reduction efficiency of 46.5% after 58 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of synthetic plastic polymer that can be degraded by certain insects, including Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic material that can be degraded by certain insect larvae, including superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic that is used in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam, also known as PS, is a type of plastic that can be degraded by superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic material used in the experiment, including both EPS and XPS types."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic material that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic product that is widely used in packaging and is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a diet with almost zero nutritional value, causing high mortality in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of feed used for mealworm larvae, resulting in the lowest value of fatty acid content."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a chemical property that is degraded by microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8f677b3b4d06e07d3a58446375133fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c16f995987af72f86486559556052e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene foam is a type of plastic consumed by superworms and yellow mealworms, with evidence of oxidation and de-polymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene foam is a type of plastic that undergoes oxidation and depolymerization in the larval guts of superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene foam is a type of plastic waste that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor, with a mass reduction efficiency of 69.7% after 58 days."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC CONSUMPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic consumption is a biological process by which mealworms break down and consume plastic, potentially reducing plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption is a biological process by which superworms and yellow mealworms break down and utilize plastic as a nutrient source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption is the process by which mealworm larvae eat and break down plastic, with varying rates and efficiencies depending on the type of plastic and the presence of additional substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption refers to the process by which T. molitor breaks down and degrades plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption refers to the ability of an organism, such as Tenebrio molitor, to eat and digest plastics, such as PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption refers to the process by which mealworms ingest and degrade plastics, which is influenced by the presence of bran in their diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption refers to the process by which mealworms consume and break down different types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption refers to the process by which mealworms consume and degrade plastics, including polystyrene, polypropylene, and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which mealworms consume and digest plastics is a key biological process studied in the research."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DE-POLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"De-polymerization is a chemical reaction that occurs in the breakdown of plastic by superworms and yellow mealworms, resulting in the breakdown of polymer chains."</data>
