    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SP. YP1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus sp. YP1 can remove approximately 10.7% of LDPE films after 60 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain that can degrade LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain that can degrade polyethylene, growing on it as a sole carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a type of bacteria that has been found to degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain that has been isolated from the intestine of the mealworm and has been found to have PE-degrading activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain that is capable of degrading PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene-degrading bacterium that has been isolated from the gut of plastic-eating waxworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain that can degrade polyethylene (PE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterium capable of degrading polyethylene, with a complete genome sequence available."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the gut of waxworms, which is similar to Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a type of bacteria that can degrade commercial LDPE film with a high molecular weight."</data>
