    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Functional groups are chemical groups that can be formed or modified during the degradation of PS, with changes in functional groups observed during the passage of PS through the mealworm gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are chemical groups that can be used to study plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are the specific groups of atoms within molecules that determine their chemical properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are chemical properties of polystyrene (PS) that are being analyzed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups refer to the specific groups of atoms within a molecule that determine its chemical properties, used to evaluate the biodegradation of PE-MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups refer to the specific groups of atoms within the molecules that determine their chemical properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LLDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LLDPE, or linear low-density polyethylene, is a type of plastic that can be degraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;LLDPE, or linear low-density polyethylene, is a type of plastic material with a linear chain and less branching compared to LDPE. It is a type of polyethylene polymer that is being studied for its biodegradation properties and potential environmental impact. LLDPE has a higher level of short chain branching than HDPE and a middle degree of crystallinity. It can be biodegraded by certain organisms, such as Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, as well as mealworms. However, its biodegradability is influenced by physical properties such as molecular weight and crystallinity, making it less degradable than LDPE in some cases. LLDPE undergoes biodegradation and depolymerization when ingested by Tenebrio species, with a lower rate of degradation compared to LDPE. Despite its potential for biodegradation, LLDPE is also considered difficult to biodegrade due to its high molecular weight and hydrophobicity. Additionally, LLDPE has been found to cause oxidative stress and ecotoxicity in certain organisms. LLDPE is commonly used in packaging and other applications, and its effects on the gut microbial community of certain species, such as Tenebrio species, have been studied. Overall, LLDPE is a complex material with varying degrees of biodegradability, depending on the specific conditions and organisms involved.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">EPS, or Expanded Polystyrene, is a type of plastic material that is being studied for its biodegradability and effects on microorganisms. It refers to Expanded Polystyrene Styrofoam, a type of plastic material that is being researched for its degradation by microorganisms. EPS is a spherical plastic containing an effervescent gas and has a complex structure that makes it difficult to degrade. EPS is commonly used in various applications, including packaging materials, such as electronic packaging containers, cups, and construction industry for insulating walls, floors, and other uses. 

EPS can be degraded by certain microorganisms, such as those in the Enterobacteriaceae family, and certain bacteria, including those within the Enterobacter genus. The biodegradation of EPS is mediated by enzymes such as CES and GSTs and is also affected by the presence of other substances like bran. EPS has been shown to have a degradation rate of 48.2% when consumed by mealworms and beetles. Additionally, EPS is being used as a feedstock in the biodegradation process and is being tested for its effects on microorganisms, including L. taiwanensis, L. garvieae, Cronobacter sakazakii, and Lactococcus garvieae. 

EPS can also be used as a sole carbon source by certain bacteria, and its presence can affect the population density of various species and the fatty acid degradation pathway. It is available in different types, such as white EPS and grey EPS, and is being used as a biological marker to measure consumption rates by mealworms, with different pre-treatments affecting its consumption. Furthermore, EPS is also used to prepare PS film for microbial degradation. EPS is fed on by various insects, including the yellow mealworm, Tenebrio molitor, and larvae of Coleoptera and Lepidoptera, which can consume and break down the material.

Although EPS is not a good nutrient for mealworms, its digestion increases the portion of EPS-degrading microorganisms in the intestine of mealworms. In some contexts, EPS may also refer to a solution of polystyrene in chloroform, used in the study of polystyrene degradation, or to exopolysaccharide, a substance that larvae can feed on. However, in the context of EPS being discussed, it primarily refers to Expanded Polystyrene. Overall, EPS is a type of plastic</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
