    <node id="&quot;FTIR SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of spectroscopy that is used to analyze the chemical composition of a material, such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectra is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are a measure of the infrared radiation absorbed or emitted by a substance, used to analyze the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is a biochemical measure used to analyze the composition of feedstock and frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra refer to the infrared spectra of a material, which can provide information about its molecular structure and composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra, or Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of materials, including polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is a technique used to analyze the degradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are used to analyze the functional groups present in the frass of PS- and CS-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is a biochemical measure used to analyze the surface morphology of PS/CS samples treated with or without Lac640 enzyme."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is a measure used to analyze the chemical composition of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are a biochemical measure used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene, showing chemical changes and incorporation of oxygen in the PS polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"The FTIR spectra is a measure of the molecular structure of the PP foam and frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of LDPE and its biodegradation products."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are a measure of the chemical composition of a material, used to analyze the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBONYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of functional group that is thought to contribute to the biochemical oxidation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that can be detected in the FTIR spectra of frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that contains a carbon-oxygen double bond."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that is thought to contribute to the biochemical oxidation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that is formed during the biodegradation of HDPE, indicating the starting step in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that is found in the frass of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of chemical group that is present in the egested frass of A. grisea larvae after PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are functional groups that are formed during the biodegradation of polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of chemical property that is being measured in the experiment, with an increase in signals associated with carbonyl groups indicating degradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that is formed during the degradation of polystyrene, which can influence the chemical composition of the plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are a type of functional group that are formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, resulting in the incorporation of oxygen into the PS polymer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydroxyl groups are a functional group that can be modified through oxidation, leading to the degradation of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group that contains a hydrogen-oxygen bond."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group present in the biodegradation products of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group that can be detected in the FTIR spectra of frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group that is associated with the FTIR spectra of PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of chemical structure found in polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymers after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are detected on the surface of PS films after biodegradation, indicating oxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of chemical property that is being measured in the experiment, with shifting associated with hydroxyl groups indicating degradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group that is formed during the degradation of polystyrene, which can influence the chemical composition of the plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group that are formed during the biodegradation of polystyrene, resulting in the incorporation of oxygen into the PS polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups refer to a type of functional group that is observed in the FTIR spectra of degraded PP polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are a type of functional group that is present in the biodegraded LDPE, as shown by the FTIR spectra."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methylene is a chemical property that can be modified through oxidation, leading to the degradation of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Methylene is a type of functional group that contains a carbon-hydrogen bond."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PDI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PDI is a biochemical measure representing the polydispersity index of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI is a measure of the polydispersity index of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI is a physical property of polypropylene that affects its biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI is the polydispersity index, a measure of the molecular weight distribution of PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI stands for Polydispersity Index, which is a measure of the molecular weight distribution of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI, or polydispersity index, is a measure of the molecular weight distribution of a substance."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI, or polydispersity index, is a physical property of polymers that changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PDI is a measure of the polydispersity of polymer chains, and it is used to study the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mn is a biochemical measure representing the number-average molecular weight of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a biochemical measure that represents the number-average molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer, used to study the degradation of PS microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is an element with minor content in PS and PU1."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn refers to the number-average molecular weight of a polymer, which can decrease during the degradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn stands for Number-Average Molecular Weight, which is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, or number-average molecular weight, is a physical property of polymers that changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, or number-average molecular weight, is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer, such as polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn refers to the number-average molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is the number-average molecular weight, which declines in the extract of frass compared to Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is the number-average molecular weight, used to characterize the molecular weight distribution of the control PS and the fecula."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a chemical element present in the frass, with an average proportion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the molecular weight of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer, used to assess biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the number-averaged molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn refers to the number-average molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics, which affects their degradation and interaction with the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the molecular weight of PS films, detecting changes after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, or number average molecular weight, is a measure of the molecular weight of the surgical mask material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, or number-average molecular weight, is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, or number-averaged molecular weight, is a measure of the molecular weight of the degradation products of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn refers to the number-average molecular weight, with changes in Mn indicating depolymerization of PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a measure of the number-average molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn refers to the number-average molecular weight of PS MPs, which affects their digestion and degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mw refers to the weight-average molecular weight of PS MPs, which affects their digestion and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;MW refers to the molecular weight of a polymer, specifically the weight-average molecular weight, which is a physical property that changes during biodegradation. It is a measure of the molecular weight of various polymers, including polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polystyrene (PS), and polyethylene (PE). MW is used to study the degradation of these polymers, including microplastics, and to assess their biodegradation. The weight-average molecular weight is also used to characterize the molecular weight distribution of polymers. In the context of biodegradation, changes in MW can indicate depolymerization of polymers such as polypropylene (PP) and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). Additionally, MW is used to evaluate the impact of plastic on insect metabolism, particularly in the study of mealworms. Overall, MW is a biochemical measure that represents the weight-average molecular weight of a polymer, providing valuable information on the physical properties and degradation of various polymers.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IDEONELLA SAKAIENSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ideonella Sakaiensis is a bacterium that degrades plastic, specifically poly(ethylene terephthalate) (PET)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis is a microorganism that is capable of degrading poly(ethylene terephthalate)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis is a bacterial species that can degrade PET, with potential applications in biotechnology and plastic waste management."</data>
