    <node id="&quot;INSECTS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41af774fc28352c7dd95d8c3cb43f855&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e9eb740edf53e540b468122b302cb82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d</data>
      <data key="d1">Insects are a class of invertebrate animals that include a diverse range of species, such as beetles, mealworms, waxworms, and dry-wood termites. They are organisms that have a diverse gut microbiota, which plays a crucial role in their ecology and evolution. Insects are capable of degrading various types of plastics, including Styrofoam, polyethylene, and polystyrene, making them a potential solution for plastic waste management. Specifically, insects such as the wax moth, mealworms, and T. molitor larvae have been found to biodegrade these plastics through enzymatic activities. The digestive system microbiota of insects plays a crucial role in the degradation process. Insects are also being studied for their ability to break down and modify the backbone of various plastics. Furthermore, insects are considered a potential food and feed source, with various species being studied for their nutritional value and environmental impact. They are a subject of study in the context of biodegradation, particularly in the context of frass production, and their biomass is being analyzed. Insects are also used as a diet for farmed fish, and their life cycle and nutritional content are being studied. Overall, insects are a group of organisms that are being studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics, their nutritional value, and their role in the ecosystem. Researchers, such as Brandon et al., have studied the ability of insects to degrade plastics, and their work has shown that insects have the potential to be a valuable tool in managing plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
    </node>
