    <node id="&quot;KLEBSIELLA OXYTOCA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a Gram-negative, rod-shaped bacterium that is closely related to K. pneumoniae and has been found to be responsible for degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium isolated from the gut of T. molitor worms, capable of degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor, which has the potential to biodegrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium that has been isolated from the gut of Tenebrio molitor and has been found to have the ability to degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium that is being studied and identified using molecular techniques such as PCR."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium that was used in the study, and its strains were analyzed for the presence of the alkB gene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a species of bacteria that includes several strains, including JCM 1665, NBRC 102593, and ATCC 13182."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a type of microorganism that is capable of degrading polystyrene, a type of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium that has been isolated and has shown biodegradable ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a type of bacteria found in the mealworm gut, capable of degrading plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a species of bacteria that has been found to be responsible for polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a species of bacteria that was identified in the gut of Tenebrio molitor and is potentially responsible for the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium that has been investigated for its role in degrading polystyrene, highlighting its potential in environmental cleanup efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella oxytoca is a bacterium found in the intestine of mealworms that digested polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b74e88d1f94354055e078b38af6a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c341ee1e0a5f54e33ca3379aa9d01c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALKB GENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"AlkB gene is a gene used for phylogenetic analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"The AlkB gene is a gene involved in the degradation of polystyrene by Klebsiella oxytoca."&lt;SEP&gt;"The AlkB gene is responsible for the degradation of polystyrene and has been found in Klebsiella oxytoca strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"The alkB gene encodes for the enzyme alkane-1-monoxygenase, which is involved in the oxidation of inactivated alkanes and is a potential marker for polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The alkB gene is a genetic marker used to detect the presence of alkane-1-monooxygenase in Klebsiella oxytoca strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cdc0cc71ed5e76f044e4db50f81ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d71b4e70d7c620ee64c709aaeae634a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33649582d350bc1da7dbca1a65092127</data>
    </node>
