    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS is a difficult-to-decompose material that is usually buried or incinerated, causing environmental pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by insects, potentially allowing for its safe use in animal or human feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS), also known as Styrofoam, is a type of plastic that has been found to be degradable by certain species of insects, such as Tribolium castaneum."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of polystyrene used in building insulation and packaging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a type of plastic that is commonly used in packaging and other applications. It is one of the major sources of plastic waste and microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a product that is used as feedstocks for the mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of polystyrene that is used as insulation material."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a durable single-use plastic that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a type of plastic used in the experiment as a diet for the mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a chemical substance that is being degraded by Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a type of Styrofoam that is used as a feed for mealworms in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene foam used in the study, with lower density and compressive strength compared to XPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene that is used in foam and acid-treated cup consumption by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a type of polystyrene foam used as a diet in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is a material provided by Woosung Resin Co., Ltd., consisting of 93-96% EPS, 4-7% pentane, and less than 1% of 1,2,5,6,9,10-hexabromocyclododecane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of polystyrene used as insulation or packaging material."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SERRATIA MARCESCENS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor, capable of biodegrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is a bacterium associated with hydrocarbon degradation and bioremediation, and is one of the most prevalent strains identified in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is a bacterium that has been reported to be a good plastic biodegrader."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is a type of bacterium that has been identified as contributing to the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is a bacterium that has been isolated and has shown biodegradable ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is a bacterium that has been studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene, particularly in the context of microbiome research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is one of the unique gut microorganisms associated with PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is a bacterium that was isolated from the digestive tracts of mealworms and was found to have polystyrene degrading activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Serratia marcescens is one of the isolated species that degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
