    <node id="&quot;NUTRITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrition can enhance the survival rate and polystyrene consumption rate of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition refers to the process of providing nutrients to living organisms, and is a concept being explored in relation to insect-based food sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition refers to the process of providing nutrients to living organisms, and is being investigated in the context of edible insects as a source of protein."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is the process by which organisms obtain and utilize nutrients, in this case, the mealworms' diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a supplement that enhances the rate of PE degradation in lesser waxworms when added to their diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a factor that affects the growth and development of mealworms, and plastics lack nutritional value."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a nutritional component being studied in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is essential for mealworm metabolism, with plastic polymers lacking sufficient nutritional value."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a factor that affects the rate of plastic degradation by insects such as mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is essential for the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms, and sources of nutrition such as bran and soy protein can facilitate faster rates of degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a biological concept that is related to the fatty acid content of mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is the concept of providing nutrients to Tenebrio molitor for their growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition refers to the nutrients and energy required by the yellow mealworms for growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a critical component of the diet of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae, and is necessary for their growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition refers to the nutrients required by the larvae to survive and grow."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrition is a biological concept that refers to the process of obtaining nutrients for growth and development."</data>
