    <node id="&quot;WAXWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Waxworms are a species of insect larvae that have been found to biodegrade polyethylene and other plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that are capable of eating and degrading synthetic plastics like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that are mentioned as having a similar ability to superworms in degrading plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that can break down polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that can chew and eat polystyrene films due to the presence of certain bacterial strains in their gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are organisms that are capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) and are used as a model system in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of the greater wax moth, which are capable of degrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that can degrade PE, with a species of Bacillus, YP1, isolated from their intestine."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that are mentioned as having bacterial strains that can biodegrade PE films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of the Indian mealmoth and are capable of eating polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that can eat and biodegrade polyethylene, with their gut microorganisms playing a key role in the process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that are similar to mealworms and have been found to have gut microorganisms that can degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects whose gut microbiota can degrade polyethylene, breaking down the plastic into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms, specifically the caterpillars of the wax moth Galleria mellonella, are capable of biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects whose guts contain bacteria capable of biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects whose guts contain bacterial strains capable of biodegrading polystyrene, demonstrating their importance in environmental ecology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms, specifically the larvae of Achroia grisella, have been found to have the ability to biodegrade polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that are known to biodegrade plastics, such as polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that eat plastic and have bacterials in their guts that are capable of biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of the wax moth and have been shown to biodegrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are a species of insect that has been found to biodegrade plastics, including polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
    </node>
