    <node id="&quot;DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Degradation is the biological process by which polypropylene masks are broken down by yellow mealworms, resulting in changes to the chemical composition and structure of the material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which insects break down and degrade Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which mealworms break down PS, resulting in changes in molecular weight and functional groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which mealworms break down polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process of breaking down materials, including polypropylene (PP), through biological or chemical means."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which the bacterial consortium breaks down polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the process by which strain YT2 breaks down polystyrene (PS) into smaller, more polar compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the process by which microorganisms break down complex materials, such as plastics, into simpler components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which the bacterium breaks down poly (ethylene terephthalate) into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation is the process by which polystyrene (PS) is broken down by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Degradation refers to the process by which mealworms break down PS MPs into smaller components."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d7f6907ee7ce47b00537b1458fcc029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1</data>
    </node>
