    <node id="&quot;METABOLOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolome refers to the complete set of metabolites in a cell or organism, which can provide information on metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolome refers to the complete set of metabolites present in a biological system, such as the metabolites produced by an insect as it breaks down PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"The metabolome is a measure of the metabolic pathways and processes that occur in the gut microbiome of T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolome refers to the complete set of metabolites produced by an organism, such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, during a particular period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The metabolome refers to the complete set of metabolites present in the mealworms, which is affected by the biodegradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET-1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET-1 is a type of PET polymer that can be degraded by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET-1 is a type of PET polymer with a molecular weight of 24.83 kDa and crystallinity degree of 22.8 ± 3.06%."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET-1 is a type of PET polymer with higher crystallinity degree and MW than PET-2."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET-2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET-2 is a type of PET polymer that can be degraded by T. molitor larvae, with a slightly higher depolymerization extent than PET-1."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET-2 is a type of PET polymer with a molecular weight of 18.59 kDa and crystallinity degree of 18 ± 2.25%."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET-2 is a type of PET polymer with lower crystallinity degree and MW than PET-1."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CRYSTALLINITY DEGREE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Crystallinity degree is a measure of the degree of crystallinity in a polymer, with higher values indicating greater stability and resistance to degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity degree is a physical property being studied in relation to the biodegradation of PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity degree is a physical property of polyethylene microplastics that affects their biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity degree is a physical property of polymers that affects their biodegradability, with amorphous regions being more susceptible to microbial attacks than crystalline regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity degree is a physical property that affects the biodegradation of PET polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity degree refers to the degree of crystallinity in the polyethylene microplastics, which affects their degradation and interaction with the gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
