    <node id="&quot;SPCR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SPCR is a biochemical measure used to calculate the specific consumption rate of polypropylene by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"SPCR is a measure of the specific removal rate of PET microplastics by the T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"SPCR, or specific PS consumption rate, is a measure of the rate at which the larvae consume PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"SPCR refers to the rate at which mealworms consume and digest PS MPs, varying depending on the molecular weight of the PS MPs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c5fc4d4324334254706d6d5f9312276</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPCRS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SPCRs, or specific plastic consumption rates, are a measure of the rate at which T. molitor larvae consume PET, indicating their adaptation to the PET diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET-1 AND PET-2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET-1 and PET-2 are two types of PET polymers being used in the experiment, with different consumption rates and degradation efficiencies by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HFIP EXTRACTABLE FRACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HFIP extractable fraction is a measure of the amount of undigested and partially digested PET residues in the frass of the larvae, indicating the degradation efficiency of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PUR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PUR (Polyurethane) is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by insects, including Tenebrionidae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR is a type of microplastic that is being mentioned as a potential future study subject."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR is a type of plastic polymer, specifically polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR is a type of plastic that is being compared to PET in terms of biodegradation by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR is a type of plastic that is biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR, also known as polyurethane, is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR, or polyurethane, is a type of plastic material that may be degraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR, or polyurethane, is a type of petroleum-based plastic that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PUR is a type of hydrolyzable polymer that is more susceptible to biodegradation than non-hydrolyzable plastics like PE and PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NPS is a chemical substance that is being used to assess the degradation abilities of the bacterial isolates."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS is a nutrient that is degraded by microorganisms, with a significant reduction in particle count and size after treatment with Achromobacter xylosidans M9."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to a type of plastic material, likely polystyrene, that is being studied for its degradation by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to nanoplastic particles that are being studied for their biodegradation by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS, or nano-polystyrene, is a type of plastic synthesized using the nanoprecipitation method."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs are nanoplastic particles that can be ingested by Tenebrio larvae, causing oxidative stress and ecotoxicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs, or nanoparticles, are small plastic particles that can induce physiological stress and toxicity in organisms, but were not detected in the excrement of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs, or nanoplastics, are a type of toxic substance that may be produced during the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs, or nanoplastics, are not found in the frass of T. molitor larvae after they digest PET polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs refer to nanosized plastic particles that are produced during the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOTA ADAPTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbiota adaptation is a biological process by which the gut microbiota of T. molitor larvae adapt to the PET diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive system is a biological concept referring to the system by which T. molitor larvae break down and absorb nutrients from their food."&lt;SEP&gt;"The digestive system of T. molitor larvae, including enzymes and digestive fluid, plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive system refers to the system of organs and processes that allow mealworms to break down and absorb nutrients from their food."&lt;SEP&gt;"The digestive system of the insects is affected by the different diets and the presence of polyethylene, leading to changes in the microbiome and the insects' physiology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive system is the system by which T. molitor larvae break down PET polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Digestive system is the system responsible for breaking down food, including PP, in the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MASS REDUCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mass reduction is a biochemical measure of the amount of PET that is broken down by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass reduction is a biological process that occurs in mealworm insects as they consume plastic, with varying degrees of mass loss depending on the type of plastic and the presence of additional substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass reduction is a measure of the amount of PET polymer that is biodegraded."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET polymers are a type of product that can be degraded by T. molitor larvae, with a significant reduction in molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET polymers are the molecules that make up PET, with different molecular weights being tested for their effect on biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IDEONELLA SAKAIENSIS 201-F6&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium that can degrade PET polymers, but with a lower efficiency than T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium that is capable of degrading PET, but at a slower rate compared to Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a species of bacteria that is known to degrade PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a type of bacterium that can biodegrade PET, but with a lower efficiency compared to T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MZ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mz is a biochemical measure representing the z-average molecular weight of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a biochemical measure that represents the z-average molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz, or z-average molecular weight, is a physical property of polymers that changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a measure of the molecular weight of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer, used to assess biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a measure of the size-averaged molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz refers to the z-average molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics, which affects their degradation and interaction with the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a measure of the molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a measure of the size-average molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz is a measure of the z-average molecular weight of a polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mz refers to the size-average molecular weight of PS MPs, which affects their digestion and degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1H NMR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1H NMR (Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) is a technique used to analyze the chemical structure of substances, in this case, the residual polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR (Proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) is a technique used to analyze the physiochemical properties of substances, including the biodegradation of polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a biochemical measurement technique used to analyze the changes in physiochemical properties of the egested frass of the lesser waxworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a chemical property that is used to analyze the composition of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a proton nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy technique used to characterize the functional groups of PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a technology used to analyze the degradation of PET polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR, or proton nuclear magnetic resonance, is a process used to analyze the chemical structure of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR stands for Proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance, a biochemical measure used to analyze the chemical composition of the polystyrene microplastics (MPs)."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of polystyrene residues, revealing new peaks associated with the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a technique used to analyze the chemical composition of materials, in this case, the degradation of PP polymers by T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a type of analysis used to study the biodegradation of PP by T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"1H NMR is a biochemical measure used to analyze the chemical composition of LDPE polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradability is the ability of a substance to be broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the ability of a substance to be broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the ability of a material to be broken down by microorganisms, in this case, the biodegradability of PE mulching films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the ability of a substance to be broken down by living organisms, involved in the biodegradation of PP polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the ability of a material to be broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the ability of microorganisms, such as mealworms, to break down organic materials, including PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER BIODEGRADABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer biodegradability is the ability of a polymer to be broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer biodegradability refers to the ability of a polymer to be broken down by biological organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer structure is the arrangement of molecules in a polymer, which can change during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT REDUCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the change in molecular weight of a material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight reduction is the decrease in molecular weight of a polymer during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight reduction is a measure of the decrease in molecular weight of PE during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEPOLYMERIZATION PATTERN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Depolymerization pattern is a biological concept that refers to the changes in molecular weight distribution of a polymer during depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization pattern is the way in which a polymer breaks down into smaller molecules during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization pattern is a biological process that occurs during the biodegradation of plastics, with different patterns for different types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization pattern is a measure of the way in which PET polymers are broken down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization pattern is a chemical property of the PS polymers, which is analyzed using Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AROMATIC BOND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aromatic bond is a type of chemical bond found in polymers, which can be broken during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ETHER BOND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ether bond is a type of chemical bond found in polymers, which can be broken during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBONYL BOND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbonyl bond is a type of chemical bond found in polymers, which can be broken during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl bond is a type of chemical bond that is formed during the biodegradation of LDPE, as shown by the FTIR spectra."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BENZENE RING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Benzene Ring is a functional group that is destroyed by the gut microbes of yellow mealworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene ring is a chemical property of PS and lignin that is cleaved by enzymes such as alkylglycerol monooxygenase and peroxidase."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene ring is a chemical property that is present in PS microplastics and is affected by metabolism by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene ring is a type of functional group found in polymers, which can be broken during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene ring is a type of chemical structure found in polystyrene foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene ring is a chemical property of polystyrene that is affected during biodegradation, resulting in the breakdown of the PS polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"The benzene ring is a structural component of polystyrene that can be broken down by certain microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbiomes are associated with PET degradation, with certain bacteria such as Enterococcus sp. and Spiroplasma sp. being dominant genera."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes of mealworms play a synergistic role in the biodegradation of PET intermediates and provide nitrogen sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes refer to the community of microorganisms that live in the gut of the larvae, and their composition and function were analyzed in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes is a biological concept that refers to the community of microorganisms living in the gut of insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterococcus sp. is a bacterial genus associated with LDPE biodegradation in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is a bacterium that has been studied for its ability to degrade polystyrene, particularly in the context of microbiome research and environmental cleanup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is a genus of microorganisms that is involved in the degradation of lignocellulose and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is a plastic-degrading genus that is enriched or isolated from environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus sp. is a common gut-associated bacterium in insects, including mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Spiroplasma sp. is a bacterial genus associated with LDPE biodegradation in the gut of T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a bacterium found in the gut microbiome of T. molitor, generally considered a pathogen in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a common gut-associated bacteria in T. molitor larva gut microbiota associated with plastic-degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a dominant bacterial genus related to PET biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a type of bacteria that is generally regarded as a pathogen or male-killing bacterium in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of microorganisms that is involved in the degradation of lignocellulose and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is a genus of bacteria that is found in the gut microbial community of larvae fed with PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasma sp. is one of the majority members of the gut microbiome in mealworms, common in insect guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIBIOTIC SUPPRESSION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antibiotic suppression is a method used to examine the depolymerization and biodegradation of PET in the presence of antibiotics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antibiotic suppression is a method used to inhibit the growth of microorganisms, which can affect the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antibiotic suppression is a process used to impair the capability of mealworms for PS biodegradation and mineralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antibiotic suppression refers to the use of antibiotics to inhibit the growth of microorganisms, which can affect the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antibiotic suppression refers to the use of antibiotics to suppress the growth of microorganisms, including gut microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The use of antibiotics to suppress the growth of gut microbes in Tenebrio obscurus and Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER CHAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer chains are the components of PET polymers that are broken down during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer chains refer to the molecular structure of polypropylene, which is affected by oxidation and degradation during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer chains are the molecular structure of plastics, and they are affected by the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer chains are the molecular structures of polymers, which are broken down during biodegradation."</data>
