    <node id="&quot;WATER CONTACT ANGLE (WCA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Water contact angle (WCA) is a measure of the degree to which a surface repels water, with higher values indicating greater hydrophobicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle (WCA) is a measure of the hydrophobicity of a surface."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle (WCA) is a measure of the surface hydrophobicity of degraded Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle (WCA) is a method used to monitor changes in hydrophobicity on polymer surfaces and assess biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle (WCA) is a technique used to analyze the surface hydrophobicity of plastic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle is a measure of the hydrophobicity of a surface, and is being used to study the surface properties of polystyrene film sheets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle (WCA) is a physical property used to investigate the physical and chemical changes in degraded PS films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water contact angle is a measure of the hydrophobicity of PE polymers."</data>
