      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is a technique used to identify new functional groups in either liquid or solid samples during microbial biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEGRADING BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms that are capable of breaking down plastic polymers like polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-degrading bacteria, such as Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2 and Enterobacter absburiae strain YT1, are capable of degrading plastics, including polystyrene and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-degrading bacteria are microorganisms that are capable of degrading plastics such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-degrading bacteria are the target of the study, aiming to isolate and identify them in the gut microbiome of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1,2 DICHLOROBENZENE (DCB)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,2 Dichlorobenzene (DCB) is a chemical used for the GPC analysis of low density polyethylene (LDPE) and polypropylene (PP)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DICHLOROMETHANE (DCM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dichloromethane (DCM) is a chemical used for the extraction of LDPE, PP, and polylactic acid (PLA)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;JELLY POWDERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Jelly powders are used as an adhesive to make polystyrene-bran mixed diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;T. OBSCURUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"T. obscurus is a genus of mealworms that has been studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;T. obscurus is a species of darkling beetle, specifically a type of mealworm, that has been found to have the ability to biodegrade various types of plastics, including low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), and polystyrene (PS). The species is capable of consuming and biodegrading these plastics, exhibiting oxidative stress responses and ecotoxicity. T. obscurus has been compared to T. molitor in terms of its ability to degrade certain materials, and has been found to have a more efficient biodegradation process for polystyrene. The species is also being studied for its ability to depolymerize polymers, specifically LLDPE and HDPE, and has been found to have a higher PS consumption ability and superior PS biodegradability. T. obscurus is commercially reared for animal feed and insect protein, and is used in biodegradation tests, specifically for ingesting and biodegrading microplastics. The species has been found to be capable of biodegrading microplastics, including LDPE, LLDPE, and HDPE, and has been used in experiments to study the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) polymers. The gut microbiomes of T. obscurus have been analyzed when fed with different diets, including LDPE, HDPE, LLDPE, and wheat bran. T. obscurus has been observed to have a unique gut microbiome similar to T. molitor, and its larvae contribute to the biodegradation of LDPE. The species has been found to have a lower specific LDPE consumption rate than T. molitor, but is capable of depolymerizing LDPE in a gut-microbe independent process. Overall, T. obscurus is a species of insect that has the potential to play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics, and is an important subject of study in the field of biodegradation.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099f2d6fd3abc2f73550f579999b13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225</data>
    </node>
