    <node id="&quot;PS MPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS MPs (Polystyrene Microplastics) are a product that is being exposed to earthworms, causing a significant increase in ROS levels."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS MPs are a type of microbe that can cause oxidative stress in T. molitor larvae, with biodegradation occurring via oxidative depolymerization processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS MPs refer to polystyrene microplastics, a type of microplastic used in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS MPs are the polystyrene microplastics used as feedstock for the mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS MPs refer to the polystyrene microplastics used as a food source for the mealworms, with varying molecular weights."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbiome analysis is the process of studying the community of microorganisms living in the gut of the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiome analysis is the study of the microorganisms present in the gut of insects such as Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSIOLOGICAL PARAMETERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physiological parameters refer to the physical and biological characteristics of the larvae, such as survival rate, cannibal rate, and pupation rate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANNIBAL RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cannibal rate is a measure of the frequency at which microorganisms, such as Z. atratus larvae, exhibit cannibalism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibal rate is a physiological parameter measured in the larvae, indicating the percentage of larvae that engage in cannibalism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibal rate is the physiological response of the larvae to the feedstock, measured as the percentage of larvae that engage in cannibalism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibal rate refers to the percentage of T. molitor larvae that engage in cannibalistic behavior after ingesting PS foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibal rate refers to the rate at which the larvae exhibit cannibalism behavior, with Z. atratus having a higher cannibal rate."</data>
