      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PUR is a type of plastic that is widely used and has a different structure than PE and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic mentioned as being biodegradable by the larvae of T. molitor and Z. atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by certain insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a type of plastic that is commonly used in packaging materials and is known to be resistant to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that is commonly used in packaging and other applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that is widely used in various applications, including packaging materials and foams."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that is resistant to biodegradation and can cause physiological stress responses in macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by yellow mealworms, with a range of properties and applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that is widely used and is mentioned as a comparison to polypropylene (PP)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by certain microorganisms, such as the yellow mealworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of petro-based polymer mentioned as a potential area of future research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of petro-based polymer that may be studied in future research on plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of polymer that can be degraded by microbial cultures."</data>
