    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Clostridium is a bacterium found in the gut microbiome of T. molitor, potentially involved in the biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a bacterium that can be found in the gut microbiome of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor, involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a type of anaerobic bacteria that is found in higher presence in Diet 3."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a type of bacteria that is present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria that is part of the intestinal microbial ecology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a type of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of the Tenebrio species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria that is present in the posterior gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria that was found to have a significant difference in relative abundance between the bran and PE diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria that has been identified in the gut of mealworms and plays a role in the biodegradation of styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria that is present in the gut of mealworms and is capable of living in anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a bacterium that has been isolated from the guts of various nitrogen-fixing insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a genus of bacteria that has been found to have nitrogen-fixing abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium is a type of bacteria that can degrade hemicellulose and polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene is a chemical compound involved in the degradation pathway of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a chemical compound that can be degraded by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a chemical property that is formed during the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a monomer that makes up PS, with its degradation affected by the presence of bran and water."&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a chemical compound that plays a significant role in the production and degradation of polystyrene (PS), a type of plastic material. It is a monomer used to manufacture polystyrene through a free radical polycondensation reaction and is also a component of polystyrene that can be identified in various analyses. Styrene is used in the production of plastics, such as polystyrene foam, and is also a decomposition product of polystyrene, detected in the FTIR spectrum of Styrofoam. It is the main thermal decomposition product of PS, detected in the TG-FTIR analysis of Styrofoam. Styrene is also a chemical compound that can be metabolized by bacteria, biodegraded by microorganisms, such as Pseudomonas sp. strain Y2 and Bacillus, through aerobic and anaerobic degradation pathways, and decomposed by microbial decomposition. Various microorganisms and enzymes are capable of degrading styrene, indicating its potential for environmental remediation. However, styrene is also considered a toxic compound that is produced during the biodegradation of plastics and can be toxic to humans and wildlife. Additionally, styrene may be present in biomass, with a potential role in the degradation of plastic packaging, and is used as a standard in the targeted analysis of monomers and oligomers. Overall, styrene is an important chemical compound that is associated with the degradation of polystyrene and has significant implications for environmental remediation and the production of plastic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a monomer used to produce polystyrene through polymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a chemical compound that is used to make polystyrene, and it is influenced by the ability of microorganisms to biodegrade the plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a chemical compound that can be catabolized by certain microorganisms, such as Pseudomonas fluorescens."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a chemical compound that is related to polystyrene and is involved in its degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
