    <node id="&quot;CRONOBACTER SAKAZAKII&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cronobacter sakazakii is a newly proposed EPS-degrading bacterium that increased significantly in the EPS feeding group population."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cronobacter sakazakii is a species found in both the bran-fed and EPS-fed groups, with population densities of 0.77% and 25.47%, respectively, and is suggested to be involved in EPS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS GARVIEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lactococcus garvieae is a newly proposed EPS-degrading bacterium that increased significantly in the EPS feeding group population."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus garvieae is a species found in both the bran-fed and EPS-fed groups, with population densities of 4.82% and 36.86%, respectively."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPS DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS degradation is a biological process by which mealworms break down EPS, with a degradation rate of 48.2%."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS degradation is a biological process that involves the breakdown of EPS by microorganisms, such as Cronobacter sakazakii and Lactococcus garvieae."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS degradation refers to the process of breaking down EPS, which is facilitated by mealworms and their intestinal microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS degradation is a biological process that increases the portion of EPS-degrading microorganisms in the intestine of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS degradation is the process by which microorganisms break down expanded polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL HORMONES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental hormones are a type of pollutant that can be released from EPS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE ([-CH(C6H5)CH2 -]N)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene is a chemical compound that is difficult to biodegrade due to its high molecular weight and strong structural stability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
