    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTERIALES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORDER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterobacteriales are an order of bacteria that are commonly found in the digestive system microbiota, with their presence associated with various metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is a type of microorganism involved in pesticide degradation, lignocellulose breakdown, and the metabolism of aromatic compounds and heavy metals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria that is enriched in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed with PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order that includes families such as Enterobacteriaceae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria that is found in the gut microbiome, with a mean abundance of 4.5%."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of microorganisms found in the microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is the order to which the family Erwiniaeae belongs, a large and diverse group of bacteria within the class Gammaproteobacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria that includes genera such as Klebsiella, Mixta, Kluyvera, and Citrobacter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria that includes the genus Klebsiella, which is found to be an important nitrogen-fixing symbiont of Styrofoam-eating mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
