    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTICS (MPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that are less than 5 millimeters in size. They are a major source of plastic pollution in the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that can be biodegraded by the larvae of T. molitor and Z. atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and other macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are pollutants that can be found in various environments, including marine, soil, and landfill leachate, and can have harmful effects on the environment and biota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that can have lethal effects on humans and wildlife, and can act as vectors to inorganic and organic pollutants."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTICS (NPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nanoplastics (NPs) are small plastic particles that are less than 1 micrometer in size. They are a major source of plastic pollution in the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics (NPs) are small plastic particles that can be produced through the biodegradation of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are pollutants that can be found in various environments, including marine, soil, and landfill leachate, and can have harmful effects on the environment and biota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE-1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE-1 is a product used as a feedstock for the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE feedstock used in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE foam that contains pink color additives."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE foam that is consumed and depolymerized by Tenebrio molitor larvae, with a depolymerization pattern similar to that of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam with a lower average molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) polymer being tested for depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of plastic foam being tested for biodegradation by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE foam used as a control sample."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE polymer that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE polymer that is being studied for its biodegradation properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of LDPE that is used to test the consumption and biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of plastic that undergoes biodegradation, similar to LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of polymer that can be depolymerized by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE-1 is a type of polymer that is being compared to LDPE in terms of biodegradation."</data>
