    <node id="&quot;DEPOLYMERIZATION PROCESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Depolymerization process is the first and essential step of the biodegradation of plastics, including PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization process is the process by which Styrofoam fragments are broken down into small-molecule products, catalyzed by extracellular enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization process is the process by which microorganisms break down plastics into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization process is the process by which polymers are broken down into smaller molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization process is the series of events by which polyethylene polymers are broken down into smaller molecules, resulting in a decrease in molecular weight and an increase in hydrophilicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization process is the series of steps by which polymers are broken down into smaller molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"The depolymerization process refers to the breakdown of polymers into smaller units, which is an important step in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerization process is the process by which LDPE is broken down into smaller molecules by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS polymers are products with different molecular weights that can be biodegraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS polymers are the materials being biodegraded by the T. molitor larvae, with their molecular weights analyzed through Gel Permeation Chromatography."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS polymers, including those with ultrahigh molecular weights, can be depolymerized and biodegraded by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e</data>
    </node>
