      <data key="d1">"Achroia grisella, also known as lesser waxworms, can degrade high-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene that is used in various applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic waste that can be degraded by mealworms, but less efficiently than low-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of plastic product that is being studied for its degradation by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic that is mentioned as a material that Tenebrio molitor can degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of pollutant that can be degraded by Achromobacter species."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene that is used in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic that is being studied for its biodegradation properties and potential environmental impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Density Polyethylene, also referred to as HDPE, is a type of plastic that is consumed by mealworms at a lower rate compared to polystyrene and polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic that can be consumed by mealworms, with an average consumption rate of 0.00281 mg per day per mealworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic that is less favorable for mealworm consumption, with lower average consumption rates compared to polystyrene and polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a chemical compound that can be colonized and degraded by Aspergillus niger."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of plastic that is commonly used in packaging and other applications."</data>
