    <node id="&quot;LACCASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Laccase is a lignin-degrading enzyme that can degrade polyethylene plastics with low activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme that has been found to play a role in the biodegradation of polyethylene by the actinomycete Rhodococcus ruber."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme that contributes to the biodegradation of PP polymers, working together with other enzymes such as monoxygenase and peroxidase."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme that is measured using spectrophotometry, and its activity is determined using the modified method of Dhakar and Pandey."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme involved in lignin degradation, with potential applications in biofuel production, bioremediation, and other biotechnological processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and other natural polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin and other phenolic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme involved in the degradation of lignin."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme used for lignin degradation, with a novel laccase, LacZ1, being isolated and characterized for this purpose."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme that is involved in the oxidation of aromatic compounds, and is upregulated in T. molitor larvae during PET biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Laccase is an enzyme that has been found to be involved in the degradation of synthetic textile dyes."</data>
