    <node id="&quot;LINOLEIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FATTY ACID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Linoleic acid is a metabolite that is associated with the degradation of polystyrene, with its presence indicating the potential for metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linoleic acid is a type of compound found in the diet and frass of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linoleic acid is a type of fatty acid found in the insects, with a higher content in the OAT group compared to other variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linoleic acid is a type of fatty acid that is found in the biomass of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linoleic acid is a type of fatty acid found in the larvae, with its content being measured and compared across different feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linoleic acid is a type of fatty acid with a content of 19% as reported by Dreassi et al., but its content varies in different studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PALMITIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FATTY ACID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Palmitic acid is a fatty acid that has been identified in the biodegradation of polystyrene by Achromobacter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Palmitic acid is a type of compound found in the diet and frass of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Palmitic acid is a type of fatty acid found in the insects, with a higher percentage in the OAT diet compared to other variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Palmitic acid is a type of fatty acid that is found in the biomass of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Palmitic acid is a type of fatty acid found in the larvae, with its content being measured and compared across different feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Palmitic acid is a type of fatty acid with a content ranging from 16.7 to 19.4% as reported by other authors, but its content was determined to be lower in this work."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STEARIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FATTY ACID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stearic acid is a type of compound found in the diet and frass of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Stearic acid is a type of fatty acid found in the insects, with a higher percentage in the OAT diet compared to other variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Stearic acid is a type of fatty acid found in the larvae, with its content being measured and compared across different feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Stearic acid is a type of fatty acid with a content in percentage units, usually determined by authors and measured results below 1%."&lt;SEP&gt;"Stearic acid is a type of fatty acid with the chemical formula C18:0, found in the measured fatty acids."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33</data>
    </node>
