    <node id="&quot;STYRENE MONOMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene monomers are chemical properties that are formed during the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene monomers are susceptible to biodegradation, but polystyrene is generally considered non-biodegradable due to its high molecular weight and stable structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene, synthesized through a free radical polycondensation reaction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene monomers are the building blocks of polystyrene and are involved in its degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NYSTATIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ANTIBIOTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nystatin is an antibiotic that can suppress gut microbiota in mealworms, similar to gentamicin."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTER ASBURIAE VT1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterobacter asburiae VT1 is a bacterial strain that can degrade polyethylene (PE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter asburiae VT1 is a polyethylene-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the gut of waxworms, which is similar to Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIPID BIOMASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lipid biomass is a product of the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms and their gut microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
