      <data key="d1">"Viable mealworms refer to the number of live mealworms at the end of the experimental time interval, which is used to calculate the viability rate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTESTINE MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The intestine microbiome in mealworm guts plays a crucial role in digesting plastics, with an initial hesitation to consume nutritionally depleted plastic matrices."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOREACTIVITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bioreactivity refers to the ability of enzymes to degrade synthetic polymers, with plastics containing covalent pairs of carbon bonds having low bio-reactivity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLDED PULP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molded pulp is a material used to feed mealworms before the experiment, containing natural fibers with higher nutritional value than plastic polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BAMBOO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bamboo is a natural fiber source with high nutritional value, used to feed mealworms before the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WHITE WHEAT&quot;">
