    <edge source="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;" target="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d3">208.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Tenebrio molitor larvae have a gut microbiome that is involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms, and its gut microbiome, have a collaborative relationship that enables them to create an environment conducive to plastic biodegradation. The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of various types of plastics, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene. The insect host secretes emulsifying factors, while the gut microbiome contributes microorganisms that break down the plastic. The composition of the gut microbiome varies significantly when Tenebrio molitor is fed different diets, such as polyether-PU foam, indicating a change in the microbial community. This adaptability is essential for the biodegradation of recalcitrant plastics and other organic compounds. The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae is particularly important in their ability to adapt to different foods and degrade various types of waste. Research has shown that the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor is capable of biodegrading plastics, and its role in biodegradation processes is being studied. Overall, the relationship between Tenebrio molitor and its gut microbiome is essential for their ability to biodegrade plastics and other organic compounds, and further research is being conducted to understand the effects of the gut microbiome on the insect's health and biodegradation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a role in degrading plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a crucial role in its ability to biodegrade plastics, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polypropylene (PP)."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor plays a role in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae have a unique gut microbiome that plays a role in LDPE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae plays an important role in their ability to adapt to different foods and degrade various types of waste."</data>
