    <edge source="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;" target="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d3">81.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Tenebrio molitor can also eat and degrade polystyrene (PS), similar to Zophobas atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading PS, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae are used to break down polystyrene (PS) in the experiment, indicating a direct relationship between the two."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor, specifically its larvae (mealworms), has the ability to biodegrade polystyrene, breaking it down into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae of Tenebrio molitor are capable of biodegrading polystyrene (PS), indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae are used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor can digest polystyrene (PS), potentially due to the presence of bacteria like Bacillus and Pantoea in its digestive tract."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade Polystyrene (PS), with a moderate biodegradation rate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae can biodegrade polystyrene (PS), with previous studies showing its ability to degrade PS."</data>
