    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">333.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Polystyrene is the subject of biodegradation, a process that breaks it down."&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation and polystyrene are two closely related entities. Biodegradation is a biological process that has been studied in the context of polystyrene degradation, highlighting its potential in environmental cleanup efforts. This process can break down polystyrene, also known as PS, into smaller components, reducing its harmful effects on the environment. Microorganisms, including certain bacteria and insects such as superworms, yellow mealworms, and Tenebrio molitor, play a crucial role in biodegradation, breaking down polystyrene into simpler compounds. Mealworms, in particular, have been shown to break down polystyrene through a process of broad depolymerization, resulting in changes to the plastic's molecular weight and structure. Polystyrene, a type of plastic, is subject to biodegradation, which can help to reduce the amount of plastic debris in the environment. The effectiveness of biodegradation is affected by the type of polystyrene, and various microorganisms, including those found in the gut of certain insect species, are capable of breaking down polystyrene. Overall, biodegradation is the process by which microorganisms, including mealworms and other insects, break down polystyrene into simpler compounds, indicating a significant relationship between the biological process and the plastic material. As a result, biodegradation is being studied as a potential solution to reduce the environmental impact of polystyrene, and researchers are exploring the biodegradability of polystyrene by various microorganisms, including larvae of certain insect species and different types of bacteria.</data>
