    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;GALLERIA MELLONELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d3">80.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Galleria mellonella is a genus of insects that are used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene, with different diets affecting their activity and biodegradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella is a species of insect larvae that has been used to study plastic biodegradation, and its biodegradation process is similar to that of the mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella is capable of biodegrading long-chain hydrocarbon beeswax through its hologenome, which supports microbiota-independent metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella is capable of biodegradation.""&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella, also known as the wax worm, has been found to be capable of degrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella is involved in the biodegradation process of polyethylene, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella is capable of degrading polyethylene, which could contribute to a better understanding of biodegradation processes."</data>
