    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;T. MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d3">76.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation is the process by which T. molitor larvae break down PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene plastics occurs in the genus T. molitor, which includes yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is capable of biodegrading plastics, including PS, PU1, PU2, and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is directly involved in the biodegradation of plastic waste, showcasing its potential as a natural method for plastic disposal."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading PP through their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are capable of biodegrading polyethylene terephthalate (PET) polymers, with different biodegradation rates for different molecular weights. "&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are involved in the biodegradation process of microplastics, specifically LLDPE, LDPE, and HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor can biodegrade PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor has been studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics, indicating a strong relationship between the two entities."</data>
