    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;MINERALIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d3">105.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation and mineralization are related biological processes that occur during the breakdown of organic materials, including plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are related biological processes that occur in certain organisms, such as insects, and are being studied for their potential to break down pollutants like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is a key process in the biodegradation of plastics, and has been found to be an effective method for breaking down certain types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is a process that leads to mineralization, which is the conversion of Styrofoam into carbon dioxide and other inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are related processes, as biodegradation leads to the conversion of organic materials into inorganic materials, such as carbon dioxide (CO2)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are two related biological processes that occur in the breakdown of organic substances, such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are two related biological processes that help to break down organic matter, including plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are related biological processes, as biodegradation can lead to mineralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is the process of converting organic compounds into inorganic compounds, which is a part of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are related processes, as biodegradation can lead to mineralization, which is the breakdown of organic matter into inorganic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation and mineralization are related processes, as biodegradation can lead to mineralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which organic matter is broken down, and mineralization is the process by which it is converted into inorganic matter."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, organic breakdown"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological process, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological process, conversion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological process, decomposition"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological process, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological processes, decomposition"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological processes, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological processes, organic materials"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological processes, organic matter breakdown"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological processes, pollution"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64</data>
