    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d3">80.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation of polystyrene results in changes in molecular weight, with potential for changes in polymer structure and properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight affects the biodegradation of PE polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight affects the biodegradation of PET polymers, with lower molecular weights being more easily biodegradable."&lt;SEP&gt;"The molecular weight of polypropylene affects its biodegradation, with lower molecular weight polypropylene being more easily broken down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the average molecular weight of polymer chains, which is affected by biodegradation and measured using GPC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight affects the biodegradation of polymers, with higher molecular weight making it more difficult for microorganisms to break down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a physical property of microplastics that can be affected by biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a physical property that affects the biodegradation of polyethylene, with lower molecular weights resulting in higher biodegradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"The molecular weight of a polymer chain affects its biodegradation, with lower molecular weight fractions being degraded more rapidly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a measure of the size of a polymer, with lower molecular weights indicating greater biodegradability."&lt;SEP&gt;"The molecular weight of polystyrene affects its biodegradation in mealworms, with different molecular weights resulting in different rates of biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biochemical changes, polymer degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, physical properties"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, physical property"&lt;SEP&gt;"physical property, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"polymer properties, biodegradation"</data>
