    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;FRASS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">71.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Frass is a biological concept that refers to the waste material produced by mealworms, which is affected by the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is a waste material produced by insects that can be used to study biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the waste material produced by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae during the degradation of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the waste material produced by mealworms during the biodegradation of microplastics, containing oligomers and other breakdown products."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the waste material produced by mealworms, being studied for its relation to PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the residue produced by microorganisms such as T. molitor and Z. atratus during the biodegradation of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is a biological marker of the biodegradation process, as it is the residue left behind after the microorganisms have broken down the LDPE polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the residual material left after the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the residual fragments of plastic that are left after biodegradation."</data>
