    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">102.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Larvae are a life stage of insects that are used to study the biodegradation of polystyrene, with different diets affecting their activity and biodegradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae of insects, including superworms and yellow mealworms, play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics through their consumption and degradation activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae, such as Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae are the agents of biodegradation of polystyrene, indicating a strong relationship between the two."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae of Tenebrio molitor are the life stage responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae play a crucial role in the biodegradation of polymers, such as PS, by breaking them down into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae, such as those of Galleria mellonella and Tenebrio molitor, are capable of degrading plastics through biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biodegradation process is carried out by the larvae of Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae, such as Zophobas aratus and Tenebrio molitor, can biodegrade plastics through various biological processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae is the life stage of T. molitor and T. obscurus that is involved in the degradation of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae are capable of biodegrading plastics, including PE, LDPE, and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae are the life stage of T. molitor and T. obscurus that consumes LDPE feedstocks and breaks them down through biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, larval activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biological process, life stage"&lt;SEP&gt;"life stage, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"life stage, biological process"</data>
