    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d3">103.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation is the process by which certain bacteria break down polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which mealworms break down PS waste materials, resulting in a significant reduction in molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene (PS) can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor L., indicating a potential relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene is resistant to biodegradation, but can be broken down by mealworms through the process of depolymerization and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene undergoes biodegradation through the action of microorganisms, breaking it down into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene (PS) is biodegraded by the Tenebrio molitor larvae in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene (PS) is broken down by biodegradation, a biological process carried out by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process that affects Polystyrene, leading to its breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene (PS) is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by microorganisms, breaking it down into smaller fragments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which polystyrene (PS) is broken down by microorganisms, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which microorganisms, such as mealworms, break down PS, with a mass loss of up to 51.4% after 50 days."</data>
