    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;LDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">84.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"LDPE also undergoes biodegradation, and its properties are compared to those of HDPE in terms of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE has a higher biodegradation rate compared to HDPE and LLDPE due to its significant level of long chain branching and short chain branching."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE has been studied for its biodegradation, including its potential to be broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is being tested for biodegradation, with different rates and efficiencies for different types of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of polyethylene that has been used in experiments to study biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is being studied for its biodegradation by larvae of certain insect species."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE polymers undergo biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE, or low-density polyethylene, is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of polyethylene plastic that is being degraded by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae through the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE undergoes biodegradation by insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."</data>
