    <edge source="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">88.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation is a process that occurs in mealworms, allowing them to break down polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which mealworms break down organic materials, such as polystyrene, into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, which is a complex process involving multiple biochemical reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of biodegrading various types of plastics through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which mealworms break down plastics, including PS and PE, into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are the source of bacteria that degrade polystyrene and are involved in the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of biodegrading plastic particles, breaking them down into smaller fragments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of biodegrading polystyrene, which is an example of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are the microorganisms responsible for the biodegradation of PS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can digest and depolymerize plastics, including PE, PS, and PVC polymers, through the process of biodegradation."</data>
