      <data key="d3">74.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Biodegradation is the process by which microorganisms break down HDPE, leading to changes in its chemical structure and molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is being studied for its biodegradation by larvae of certain insect species."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene that can be biodegraded by Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus larvae, with specific effects on their physiology and the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE polymers undergo biodegradation by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE, or high-density polyethylene, is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE has a lower biodegradation rate compared to LDPE and LLDPE due to its high crystallinity and low level of short chain branching."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is another type of polyethylene plastic that is being degraded by T. molitor and T. obscurus larvae through the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE undergoes biodegradation by insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE undergoes biodegradation, which is influenced by various factors such as microorganisms and pro-oxidant additives."</data>
