    <edge source="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">244.0</data>
      <data key="d4">The GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS refers to the community of microorganisms that live in the gut of MEALWORMS and play a crucial role in the biodegradation of plastics, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). The GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS contains plastic-degrading bacteria, which are the focus of study, and is affected by the diet of MEALWORMS, including the presence of plastics and antibiotics. The composition and function of the GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS change in response to different diets, with the ingestion of certain plastics leading to an increase in certain bacterial families. The GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS is responsible for the degradation of polystyrene, with the microorganisms in the gut breaking down the plastic, and plays a crucial role in the digestion process of MEALWORMS. The GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS is also affected by factors such as nutrition and antibiotics, and is the source of PM S1, which degrades PE and PS. Furthermore, the GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS plays a role in the biodegradation of microplastics and is influenced by the molecular weight of polystyrene. Overall, the GUT MICROBIOME of MEALWORMS is a vital component of the MEALWORMS' ability to adapt to different foods and degrade plastics, highlighting the importance of microbial communities in ecosystem processes.</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, gut microbiome"&lt;SEP&gt;"diet, microbiome"&lt;SEP&gt;"gut microbiome, biodegradation, microplastics"&lt;SEP&gt;"gut microbiome, molecular weight"&lt;SEP&gt;"host-microbe interaction, gut microbiome"&lt;SEP&gt;"host-microbe interaction, symbiosis"&lt;SEP&gt;"host-microbe relationship, symbiosis"&lt;SEP&gt;"host-microbiome interaction, diet"&lt;SEP&gt;"microbial association, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"microbial communities, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"microbiome, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"microbiome, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"microbiome, mealworms"&lt;SEP&gt;"source, microbiome relationship"&lt;SEP&gt;"symbiotic relationship, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"symbiotic relationship, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"symbiotic relationship, digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"symbiotic relationship, microbiome research"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
