    <edge source="&quot;GALLERIA MELLONELLA&quot;" target="&quot;POLYETHYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">347.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Galleria mellonella, a species of wax moth, also known as the greater wax moth or wax worm, is capable of biodegrading polyethylene, a type of plastic material. The larvae of Galleria mellonella have been observed to break down polyethylene into simpler compounds, potentially producing ethylene glycol as a metabolite. This biodegradation process is supported by the unique hologenome of Galleria mellonella, which enables microbiota-independent metabolism. Additionally, the gut microbiome of Galleria mellonella plays a crucial role in the biodegradation of polyethylene, although the extent of this capability is not yet fully understood. The interaction between Galleria mellonella and polyethylene is significant, and the insect's ability to consume and biodegrade polyethylene suggests a potential relationship between the two entities. The discovery of Galleria mellonella's ability to biodegrade polyethylene is a significant finding in the field of biodegradation, and may have implications for the development of new methods for breaking down plastic waste. Overall, Galleria mellonella's capacity to biodegrade polyethylene demonstrates a remarkable relationship between the microorganism and the plastic material, and highlights the potential for further research into the biodegradation of plastics by insects and other organisms. Galleria mellonella's role in polyethylene biodegradation is an area of ongoing study, and while its capabilities are not yet fully defined, the insect has been found to be capable of degrading polyethylene, indicating a significant interaction between Galleria mellonella and polyethylene. This interaction may provide a potential solution for plastic waste management, and Galleria mellonella's ability to biodegrade polyethylene has been consistently observed, suggesting a strong relationship between the two entities.</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, bacterial contribution"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect capabilities"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect-based degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect-plastic interaction"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, microbial interaction"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, microorganisms"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic pollution"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic waste management"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, polyethylene"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, nutrition"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, polyethylene"&lt;SEP&gt;"insect degradation, plastic pollution"&lt;SEP&gt;"insect, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"insect, material"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, wax worm"</data>
