      <data key="d3">147.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms are able to degrade polyethylene, as observed in experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can biodegrade polyethylene, breaking it down into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene was utilized by mealworms in the experiment, with a surface consisting of flat valleys and flaky layers of material superimposed on one another."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms have been found to biodegrade polyethylene, breaking it down into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, specifically Tenebrio Molitor, are capable of degrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can eat plastics, including polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms have been found to consume and potentially biodegrade polyethylene, which could have implications for the management of plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene can be biodegraded by mealworms, which break down the plastic into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are able to degrade polyethylene, with a significant decrease in molecular weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene can be degraded by mealworms, resulting in a reduction in molecular weight and chemical modifications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene is being degraded by mealworms, and the rate of degradation is being studied."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene is degraded by mealworms, with nearly half of the ingested material being converted into CO2 and lower molecular weight compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can degrade polyethylene, a type of synthetic plastic, through biodegradation and enzymatic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can degrade polyethylene, a type of vinyl plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of biodegrading polyethylene, resulting in the formation of microplastics and potential nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms have been found to be capable of biodegrading polyethylene, a type of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of ingesting and biodegrading polyethylene microplastics, leading to a reduction in their size and p