      <data key="d3">78.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Polystyrene can affect the growth and development of T. molitor larvae, leading to changes in their energy density and reproduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor can digest polystyrene, and its intestinal microbiota contains bacteria that can decompose polystyrene, indicating a relationship between the insect and the plastic."::&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor consumes polystyrene, with a lower consumption rate than superworms, and has a similar ingestion preference to superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is fed polystyrene, which is then biodegraded by bacteria in its gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor, a species of mealworm, can consume polystyrene, which affects their nutritional composition and energy content."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor, or yellow mealworms, can biodegrade polystyrene, breaking it down into smaller components."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor, or mealworms, are able to biodegrade different types of polystyrene, including raw, processed, and expanded polystyrene, with the help of microorganisms in their gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is used to degrade polystyrene waste, and the study shows that the insect's productivity is affected by the type of polystyrene foam used."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor mealworms are used to degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is associated with the degradation of polystyrene, indicating its significance in the enzymatic activity of the digestive tract."</data>
