    <edge source="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">501.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms and polystyrene are closely related entities, with mealworms being capable of eating and biodegrading polystyrene, aided by gut microorganisms. Mealworms can consume and digest polystyrene, using it as a sole diet, and have been found to be able to ingest and metabolize polystyrene microplastics. The biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms can result in the production of styrene as a metabolite, and the process of biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms is significant in the context of sustainable bioremediation. Mealworms can break down polystyrene into simpler compounds, with nearly half of the ingested material being converted into CO2 and lower molecular weight compounds. Their gut microflora plays a crucial role in this process, with the intestinal microbiota containing bacteria that can decompose polystyrene. Studies have shown that mealworms have a preference for consuming polystyrene over other types of plastics, with an average consumption rate of 0.0762 mg per day per mealworm, or 7.02 to 8.70 mg per 100 mealworms per day. The type of polystyrene foam used can affect the productivity of mealworms, and they have been found to be capable of degrading different types of polystyrene, including raw, processed, and expanded polystyrene. Although some studies suggest that the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms may result in the formation of microplastics and potential nanoplastics, the overall connection between mealworms and polystyrene is one of mutual interaction, with mealworms playing a crucial role in the breakdown and degradation of polystyrene, and polystyrene serving as a potential food source for mealworms. This relationship is significant in the context of plastic waste management, as mealworms can help to manage plastic waste by biodegrading polystyrene. The biodegradation process also affects the gut microbiome and metabolome of the mealworms, highlighting the complex interaction between mealworms and polystyrene. Overall, mealworms are capable of biodegrading and mineralizing polystyrene, making them a potential solution for managing plastic waste.</data>
