      <data key="d3">144.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"PS can be biodegraded by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is a substrate used to feed T. molitor, with a unique microbial community composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor (mealworms) have various bacterial and fungal communities associated with PS diet, indicating a potential role in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor's gut microbiome is being studied for its potential to biodegrade PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is capable of consuming PS, with a significant loss of mass observed on the third day of the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor was used to utilize PS in the experiment, with a significant utilization rate."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor has a digestive tract that contains bacteria capable of degrading PS, including Bacillus and Exiguobacterium."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor consumes and degrades PS, with the efficiency of this process being studied and affected by various factors such as antibiotic treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are used to study the biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae consume and biodegrade PS, but with a lower consumption ability and biodegradability compared to T. obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor, or mealworms, are capable of biodegrading PS, or polystyrene, plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is a non-biodegradable material that can be decomposed by microorganisms in the gut of T. molitor larvae, but does not provide sufficient energy for their growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is a substrate used to feed T. molitor, with a unique microbial community composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is being fed a diet of PS, which affects its life cycle and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are affected by the presence of PS in their diet, which can lead to a decrease in their growth and survival rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are fed with PS, and its effects on their growth and development are being studied."</data>
