      <data key="d4">"Mealworms are capable of biodegrading PE through a process of limited extent depolymerization, as evidenced by GPC analysis and FT-IR analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of consuming and biodegrading PE, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can degrade PE, and the mechanism of this process is being studied using RNA sequencing and DEGs analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is degraded by mealworms, which affects their growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE was utilized by mealworms in the experiment, with a surface consisting of flat valleys and flaky layers of material superimposed on one another."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms may be capable of degrading PE, a type of plastic material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can degrade PE, resulting in a reduction in molecular weight and chemical modifications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms degrade PE, with a higher mass loss observed when cofed with PS, indicating a significant interaction between the two."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms have the ability to digest and biodegrade PE, a type of plastic product."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingest and break down PE foam, leading to the production of frass and the degradation of PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingest and digest PE, with a significant amount being mineralized, indicating a strong relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms can survive on PE microplastics, and their survival rates and body weights were affected by PE consumption."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, ingestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastics"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, growth"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic utilization, insect digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"survival, microplastics"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;" target="&quot;PS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">211.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"PS affects the survival, growth, and reproduction of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;Mealworms are capable of biodegrading Polystyrene (PS), converting it into CO2 and biomass. They can ingest and metabolize PS microplastics, with significant differences in molecular weight distribution, and their degradation efficiency is influenced by factors such as nutrient supplements and temperature. The mechanism of PS degradation by mealworms is being studied using RNA sequencing and DEGs analysis, and it is believed that host enzymes and gut microbiome contribute to the process. Mealworms have various bacterial and fungal communities associated with a PS diet, indicating a potential role in PS degradation. They can consume PS at a high rate, but it has a negative effect on their growth, indicating a complex relationship between the two entities. The consumption of PS by mealworms alters the structure of their gut microbiota and affects their growth and development. Mealworms can survive on PS microplastics, and their survival rates and body weights are affected by PS consumption. They are able to degrade PS through a process of broad depolymerization, as evidenced by GPC analysis and FT-IR analysis, resulting in a reduction in molecular weight and chemical modifications. Overall, mealworms have the ability to digest and biodegrade PS, a type of plastic product, and their relationship with PS is complex, with both positive and negative effects on their growth and development.</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, ingestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, microbial activity"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastics"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, symbiosis"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, growth"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, growth"&lt;SEP&gt;"diet, feedstock"&lt;SEP&gt;"diet, microbiota"&lt;SEP&gt;"ingestion, metabolism"&lt;SEP&gt;"metabolism, microplastics degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"nutrition, growth"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic consumption, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic degradation, insect larvae"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic utilization, insect digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"survival, microplastics"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a</data>
