    <edge source="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;" target="&quot;EPS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">139.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"EPS is a dietary substance for mealworms, and its digestion increases the portion of EPS-degrading microorganisms in the intestine of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a form of polystyrene that is more accessible to mealworms and has a higher rate of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is being degraded by mealworms, which is the focus of the biodegradability experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is consumed by mealworms at a high rate, especially when fed with bran, indicating a strong relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is degraded by mealworms, which affects their growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are able to degrade EPS, but their growth is not significantly affected by EPS consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are used to degrade EPS plastic material."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is being fed to mealworms to study its effects on their biochemical characteristics."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is used as a food source for the mealworms in the experiment, and its effects are being tested."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of breaking down EPS, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms biodegrade EPS, indicating a direct relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume EPS, with an average consumption rate of 0.21 ± 0.02 mg/d."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of feeding on and degrading EPS, indicating a relationship between the two entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are fed EPS as a diet, which may contain plastic-degrading microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms degrade EPS, a type of polystyrene foam used for packaging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are able to degrade EPS."</data>
