    <edge source="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;" target="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d3">250.0</data>
      <data key="d4">Mealworms are capable of biting, chewing, and degrading Polystyrene (PS), with their mass loss measured during experiments. They can consume and degrade PS, resulting in decreases in molecular weight and changes in functional groups observed during the degradation process. The gut microbiome of mealworms plays a crucial role in PS depolymerization and degradation. Mealworms can ingest and degrade PS, breaking it down into smaller components, and their consumption rate can be increased by the addition of certain nutrients such as bran and sucrose. The biodegradation process of PS by mealworms involves digestive biofragmentation, resulting in a significant reduction in molecular weight and an increase in PDI. Polystyrene (PS) is broken down by mealworms through the process of depolymerization and biodegradation, with different patterns of depolymerization being observed. The degradation of PS by mealworms has potential applications in waste management. Mealworms can consume PS, leaving visible holes and marks after being chewed, and they can gain mass after consuming PS for a certain period. However, the ingestion of PS by mealworms can also cause oxidative stress and damage. Overall, mealworms and Polystyrene (PS) are closely related, with mealworms having the unique ability to degrade PS, and their interaction has significant implications for biodegradation and waste management.</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, comparison"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, depolymerization"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, gut degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, gut microbiome"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, insect digestion"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, organism"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, polymer degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, waste management"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, biodegradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, compatibility"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, degradation"&lt;SEP&gt;"consumption, nutrient effect"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"degradation, feeding"&lt;SEP&gt;"ingestion, oxidative stress"&lt;SEP&gt;"plastic consumption, biodegradation"</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0c513c586aa89e48e9570525bfc5366&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16</data>
